(window.LING406_MODERN_PACKS=window.LING406_MODERN_PACKS||{})["m1-simple"]={"id":"simple","label":"Simpler · answer first, one step per sentence","revision":"ling406-modern-m1-simple-2","scripts":{"m1-lecture01-p001":[],"m1-lecture01-p002":[],"m1-lecture01-p003":[],"m1-lecture01-p004":[],"m1-lecture01-p005":[],"m1-lecture01-p006":[],"m1-lecture01-p007":[{"text":"Letters are not **phonemes** (the sound units that tell words apart). This is because each language has its own rules for turning spelling into sound. So one letter can stand for different sounds in different languages. English spelling is especially irregular.","children":[]},{"text":"Even regular spelling systems cause trouble when you compare languages. **Pinyin** is a fairly new system for writing Mandarin, and it is perfectly regular. The Mandarin phrase \"Wo qu tushuguan\" (我去图书馆) uses letters an English reader knows. But reading it with English rules gives the wrong sounds. The Chinese characters and the Pinyin write the same phrase in two different ways.","children":[]},{"text":"French spelling is also very regular. The French sentence \"Comment tu t’appelles?\" still needs the French rules to say it right.","children":[]},{"text":"Since a spelling system only works for its own language, comparing sounds has to start from what people actually say.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p008":[{"text":"The **International Phonetic Alphabet (IPA)** is one standard way to write the sounds of every language. This is because its symbols stand for how a sound is made, not for one language's spelling. So you can compare two sounds even when their normal spellings differ. You can also tell apart two sounds that normal spelling writes with the same letter.","children":[]},{"text":"The **IPA consonant chart** puts symbols in order by how the sounds are made. The rows include **plosive**, **nasal** and **fricative**. The columns include **bilabial**, **alveolar** and **velar**. Symbols near each other are made in related ways.","children":[]},{"text":"Since a symbol's position tells you how its sound relates to its neighbors, the chart is a set of comparisons. It is not just a list to memorize.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p009":[{"text":"The consonant chart is sorted by three **features** (properties of how a sound is made). They are place, manner and voicing.","children":[]},{"text":"**Place of articulation** (where the vocal tract is narrowed or closed) goes across the chart from front to back. Bilabial sounds are on the left, because they are made at the lips. Velar sounds and sounds made farther back are on the right.","children":[]},{"text":"**Manner of articulation** (how much the air is blocked) goes down the chart from closed to open. A **plosive** closes the air off completely. A **fricative** makes a narrow passage instead.","children":[]},{"text":"**Voicing** is about when the vocal folds start vibrating compared with the closure. They can start before or during the closure, or after it. Voicing shows up as pairs of symbols, and the right-hand one is voiced. So p and b share the bilabial plosive spot.","children":[]},{"text":"Since these are three separate features, two consonants can match on two and differ on the third.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p010":[{"text":"/p/ and /b/ are made in the same place and the same way, and differ only in voicing. This is because both are **bilabial plosives**, so the lips close and then let go. /p/ is not voiced, and /b/ is voiced. **Voicing** here is the timing of the vocal fold vibration compared with when the lips let go.","children":[]},{"text":"/t/ and /d/ differ in the same way. They are made at a different place than /p/ and /b/. But they still pair a voiceless stop with a voiced stop.","children":[]},{"text":"So features describe how sounds are alike and different in a fairly exact way. Since the same feature repeats across pairs, you can talk about **voiced stops** in general, like /b/, /d/ and /g/.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p011":[{"text":"Voiced and unvoiced stops are made differently in different languages, and in different spots within one language. The IPA writes this with three symbols. The first symbol, **[pʰ]**, is a bilabial plosive that is not voiced and is **aspirated** (a puff of air comes out after the lips open). The second symbol, **[p]**, is the same but with no puff. The third symbol, **[b]**, is a bilabial plosive that is voiced.","children":[]},{"text":"In English, at the start of a word, \"peer\" often starts with [pʰ]. \"Beer\" often starts with [p], which has no puff and no voicing. You can feel this by holding your hand in front of your mouth and saying both words.","children":[]},{"text":"In most kinds of French, the contrast is really [p] in \"pou\" against [b] in \"boue\".","children":[]},{"text":"Since English and French build the contrast differently, voiced and unvoiced do not mean the same timing in every language.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p012":[{"text":"Slashes and square brackets mean two different kinds of transcription. This is because they answer different questions about the same speech.","children":[]},{"text":"Slashes mark **broad transcription** (writing just the phonemes). /p/ and /b/ are broad. They keep only the differences that tell words apart in the language.","children":[]},{"text":"Square brackets mark **narrow transcription** (writing as much detail as possible). The forms [pʰ], [p] and [b] are narrow. The form [pʰ] adds that the sound has a puff of air. The form [p] adds that it has none. Narrow transcription can keep a puff even when it does not change the word.","children":[]},{"text":"There are two broad labels here but three narrow forms. Writing more detail does not make more phonemes. Since detail and word differences are separate, a **phone** (an actual sound) and a **phoneme** (a sound category) are not the same thing.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p013":[{"text":"Consonant features come from how your body makes the sound. This is because **place of articulation** names where the air gets blocked or narrowed in your vocal tract. Your lips can close together. Different parts of your tongue can touch different parts of your mouth. Each movement changes where the air gets stopped.","children":[]},{"text":"There are three places to know here. For a **bilabial** sound, the top and bottom lips close at the front of the mouth. For an **alveolar** sound, the tip of the tongue goes up to the ridge behind the upper teeth. For a **velar** sound, the back of the tongue goes up to the soft palate.","children":[]},{"text":"A full closure can happen at any of these places. So place is a different feature from manner. Since place is about the body, you find it by locating the main closure, not by looking at the letter.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p014":[{"text":"Consonant features describe where the air goes and what body parts block it.","children":[]},{"text":"A **nasal** sound sends air out through the nose while the mouth is closed somewhere. This is because the opening between the mouth and the nose stays open. So air keeps flowing even though the mouth is blocked. For a nasal made at the lips, the lips are closed and the path to the nose is open. So a nasal can be made at the same place as a stop and still send the air a different way.","children":[]},{"text":"Two other places use the teeth at the front of the mouth. For a **labiodental** sound, the lower lip touches the upper teeth. For a **dental** sound, the tongue is at the front teeth.","children":[]},{"text":"Since airflow and place are separate, you check them as two separate steps.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p015":[{"text":"Vowels are sorted three ways, by height, by front or back, and by lip rounding.","children":[]},{"text":"Height is about how close or open the vowel is. **Close** vowels usually have a higher jaw and tongue than **open** vowels. The vowel chart goes from close at the top to open at the bottom.","children":[]},{"text":"Front or back is about where the tongue sits. The tongue is usually farther forward for **front** vowels than for **back** vowels. The chart goes from front on the left to back on the right.","children":[]},{"text":"**Rounding** is whether the lips are rounded. When two symbols sit as a pair, the right one is rounded.","children":[]},{"text":"**/y/** is the vowel in French \"tu\" and Mandarin \"qu\" (去). It can sound like /u/ to English speakers, but it is not /u/. This is because /y/ is close and front like /i/. If you say /i/ and then round your lips, you get closer to /y/. Your tongue does not move back the way it does for /u/. Since only the lips changed, lip shape is a separate thing from tongue position.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p016":[{"text":"A vowel is a pattern of movement, not one fixed tongue shape. This is because the whole vocal tract shapes the sound, not just the tongue.","children":[]},{"text":"Each vowel can be marked by the center of the **tongue body**. That point captures one important way vowels differ. But the whole outline of the tongue can differ in more ways than that one point.","children":[]},{"text":"Some vowels keep moving while you say them. These are **diphthongs** (vowels that change during the sound), like the vowels in \"boat\" and \"bait\". A single point cannot tell you the path the tongue takes.","children":[]},{"text":"Since some vowels change while they are being made, you have to follow the change over the whole vowel.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p017":[{"text":"People who speak the same language can differ in which vowels they keep apart. This is because vowels change a lot across **dialects**, even within American English.","children":[]},{"text":"The example is the two vowels in the lower back corner of the vowel chart. One is the rounded /ɔ/, and the other is the open back /ɑ/. A speaker from the outer boroughs of New York City, a mid-Atlantic dialect, keeps these two vowels apart. So for that speaker, \"caught\" and \"cot\" are different words. Most people from other parts of the country do not keep them apart. So for them, \"caught\" and \"cot\" are **homophones** (words that sound exactly the same).","children":[]},{"text":"Both groups are speaking normal English. So this is a regular dialect pattern, not a mistake. Since dialects differ, you can only tell whether someone has the contrast by listening to that person.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p018":[{"text":"The **lack of invariance problem** is that one speech sound comes out physically different across speakers and situations, but listeners still understand it.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because people vary a lot. Some people say \"Aaron\" and \"Erin\" with the same vowel. A listener who keeps those vowels apart still understands them. A listener can hear a speaker's different vowels for some time and only notice when someone points it out.","children":[]},{"text":"Measured vowels show the same thing. Each vowel can be plotted by its **first formant (F1)** and **second formant (F2)** (two frequencies in the vowel, in cycles per second). Many tokens of one vowel spread over a wide area instead of one spot. The areas for neighboring vowels overlap.","children":[]},{"text":"Since no single pair of F1 and F2 values marks a vowel, listeners have to figure out the vowel from a signal that keeps changing. This problem comes up again and again.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p019":[{"text":"A **minimal pair** is two words that differ in meaning because of just one sound. Minimal pairs are how you prove two sounds are different phonemes. This is because two phones are **contrastive** when swapping one for the other changes the meaning. Contrastive phones are different phonemes.","children":[]},{"text":"The two low back vowels were proved this way. It took two words whose difference depended only on those vowels. For a speaker who keeps the vowels apart, \"caught\" and \"cot\" are a minimal pair. The consonants around the vowel stay the same, so only the vowel tells the words apart.","children":[]},{"text":"For a speaker who merges the vowels, \"caught\" and \"cot\" sound the same. So that pair does not prove two vowel phonemes for that speaker. Since the test is about meaning, two sounds being different is not enough on its own.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p020":[{"text":"Minimal pairs also show that a contrast depends on one particular feature. In English, /b/ and /p/, /t/ and /d/, and /k/ and /g/ all differ on **voicing**. This is because in each pair the place and the stop manner stay the same. Only voicing changes inside a pair.","children":[]},{"text":"Across the pairs, the place changes. /b/ and /p/ are bilabial, /t/ and /d/ are alveolar, and /k/ and /g/ are velar. The voiced and voiceless difference repeats at each place. So one feature describes all three contrasts.","children":[]},{"text":"We know this from comparing minimal pairs in each language. Just because two sounds exist does not make them a contrast in every language. Since minimal pairs link a sound difference to a word difference, they show that voicing matters for English words.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p021":[{"text":"**Allophones** are different phones that are different ways of saying the same phoneme. This is because how a phoneme is said varies a lot. Some of that variation is very predictable. It depends on where the sound is and what sounds are next to it. So it follows a pattern and is not random. It also does not change which word you are saying.","children":[]},{"text":"You can record three phrases in **Praat** (a program for recording and analyzing speech) to compare /p/. In \"pot that\", /p/ starts the word before the vowel. In \"spot that\", /p/ comes right after /s/. In \"stop that\", /p/ ends the word.","children":[]},{"text":"The puff of air and the release of /p/ can differ across the three. Since all three are still /p/, the question is how the sound changes with its spot, not whether it becomes a new word.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p022":[{"text":"The recording of \"pot that\", \"spot that\" and \"stop that\" lets you see the **allophones of /p/** over time. This is because /p/ sits in a different spot in each phrase. So the timing of the closure, the release, the puff of air and the next voiced sound can differ.","children":[]},{"text":"The recording is read in two forms. The **waveform** is on top. It gives the loudness of the signal over time. The **spectrogram** is below. It gives the energy at each frequency over time. Time runs left to right in both, so the same moment lines up top and bottom.","children":[]},{"text":"These differences still fit with all three words having the same phoneme /p/. Since a new phoneme needs a difference that changes words, these differences alone do not make new phonemes.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p023":[{"text":"An unreleased **[p̚]** and an aspirated **[pʰ]** at the end of \"stop\" do not change the meaning. So they are allophones of /p/.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because English speakers sometimes say [stɑpʰ]. They often do it when stressing the word at the end of a sentence. They also do it when trying to speak clearly, in **citation form**. But [stɑp̚] and [stɑpʰ] mean the same thing. No minimal pair can be found for these two phones here in English. **Allo** means different, so allophones are different phones for the same phoneme.","children":[]},{"text":"These forms also switch across **morphological forms** (different forms of one word). The forms are \"stop\", \"stops\", \"stopped\" and \"stopping\". Each one puts something different after the /p/. That changes whether the /p/ is released and aspirated.","children":[]},{"text":"Since hearing a difference does not mean the meaning changed, an audible difference is not always a new phoneme.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p024":[{"text":"The word voicing can be confusing because it is used in two different ways.","children":[]},{"text":"In **narrow transcription** (describing specific phones), a sound is called voiced if the vocal folds vibrate during the whole sound. This is about one real sound that someone made.","children":[]},{"text":"In **broad transcription** (describing phonemes), voicing can be **realized** (actually produced) in several different ways. One way is whether there is **aspiration** (a puff of air) at the start of a word. Another way is how long voicing lasts compared with the closure in \"writer\" and \"rider\". This is about a contrast between categories.","children":[]},{"text":"Since one phoneme contrast can show up through different physical cues, you first have to ask which level someone means by voiced.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p025":[{"text":"English speakers change sound clusters that English does not allow. This is because of **phonotactics** (roughly, which phonemes can go together in a syllable).","children":[]},{"text":"The name Sbarro is spelled with sb at the start. English speakers say [spɑroʊ] or [zbɑroʊ]. They never say [sbɑroʊ], whatever they think they are saying.","children":[]},{"text":"Foreign names can force speakers to deal with these clusters. Speakers then usually add vowels. The Polish name Zbigniew comes out as [zɨ’bIg.nʲu]. The Kru name Gbagbo comes out as [gə.bɑ.gə.boʊ]. The added vowels split the cluster into extra syllables.","children":[]},{"text":"These changes follow English syllable patterns. They do not happen because speakers cannot say each consonant. Since syllable rules are complex but still shape how we speak, spelling alone does not predict what an English speaker says.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p026":[{"text":"Phonemes are not letters, and they are not even specific sounds or movements. This is because a **phoneme** is \"the smallest phonetic unit in a language that is capable of conveying a distinction in meaning.\" So a phoneme is defined by the meaning differences it makes. That links it to minimal pairs. One phoneme can also have several allophones. Using this definition can be complicated in practice.","children":[]},{"text":"Three things are easy to mix up. A **letter** is part of a writing system. A **phone** is a sound someone actually makes. A **phoneme** is a category that tells words apart.","children":[]},{"text":"The **International Phonetic Alphabet** lets speech scientists talk about how sounds are alike and different across languages. So it is easy to think of the IPA as an alphabet of phonemes. That would be a mistake, since phonemes are not letters.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p027":[{"text":"Speech is one sound that keeps changing, not a neat row of letter-like pieces. This is because literate people get used to letters, so they imagine speech that way. But speech is nothing like that.","children":[]},{"text":"A **spectrogram** (a display of sound energy over time and frequency) of \"A sore back is a real pain\" makes this clear. Time runs left to right, and frequency goes up. Darker areas have more energy. The patterns change over time, and neighboring sounds blend into each other. The labels and boundary lines are added on top, and they do not cut the sound into pieces.","children":[]},{"text":"A phoneme category does not match one block of sound that looks the same every time. Since the sounds blend together, recognizing the sentence means making sense of changing patterns, not finding a clean edge around each letter.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p028":[{"text":"Speech is made by the lips, jaw, tongue and other parts of the vocal tract moving together. This is because their changing shape is what shapes the sound that comes out.","children":[]},{"text":"A side view of the vocal tract from USC SPAN lets you see these **articulators** (the moving parts that make speech) in action. It shows where the tongue is inside the mouth and the passage down toward the throat.","children":[]},{"text":"Each part does its own job. The **tongue** changes the shape inside the mouth. The **lips and jaw** change how open the mouth is.","children":[]},{"text":"The movements happen as one continuous sequence. So a speech **gesture** (a movement that makes a sound) is part of a moving system. Since the parts keep moving, they do not make one letter-like sound at a time.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p029":[{"text":"**Hockett (1955)** said hearing speech is like an inspector identifying eggs after they have been smashed together. This is because speech mixes its units together, but the listener only has to identify them.","children":[]},{"text":"First, a row of unboiled Easter eggs of different sizes and colors rides along a moving belt. These eggs stand for the signals from the **phoneme source**.","children":[]},{"text":"Next, the belt carries the eggs through a wringer. The rollers smash the eggs and rub them into each other. That mess stands for the output of the **speech transmitter**.","children":[]},{"text":"Last, an inspector looks at the yolks, the spread-out egg whites and the bits of shell. He figures out which eggs went into the wringer. The inspector stands for the **hearer**. He does not have to put the eggs back together, which is impossible. Since he only has to identify them, a hearer can recognize the original sounds from a mixed signal.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p030":[{"text":"Some researchers now question whether phonemes are \"real\", either psychologically or linguistically. This is because phonemes are useful for describing contrasts. But that does not tell you how people store words or recognize speech.","children":[]},{"text":"Four papers raise this question. **Lotto and Holt (2000)** wrote about the illusion of the phoneme. **Beckman and Edwards (2000)** wrote about how phonological categories develop and the primacy of lexical learning. **Port (2007)** asked how words are stored in memory, beyond phones and phonemes. **Goldinger and Azuma (2003)** wrote about puzzle-solving science in speech perception.","children":[]},{"text":"A way of describing sounds is not the same as a model of the mind. So the open question is whether the mind really uses phoneme-sized units.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p031":[],"m1-lecture01-p032":[],"m1-lecture03-p001":[],"m1-lecture03-p002":[],"m1-lecture03-p003":[],"m1-lecture03-p004":[{"text":"Knifes can be a word, because it is a form of the verb knife. This is because the form of a word depends on the job it does in the sentence. As a noun, knife has the plural **knives**. As a verb, knife has the form **knifes** after he. In the Scrabble argument, one player says the plural of knife is knives. The other player says Tony knifes Bernardo in West Side Story. Since each player is using a different word, both are right.","children":[]},{"text":"Three ideas are in this argument.","children":["**Inflectional morphology** (changing a word's form for grammar) is what makes the plural knives.","**Derivational morphology** (making a new word out of an existing one) is what makes the verb knife from the noun. This kind is **zero derivation** (the part of speech changes and no affix is added).","**Phrases and clauses** decide the ending. \"He knifes him\" has a singular noun, then a verb, then a noun. The subject is singular, so the verb has to take the **third person singular** ending."]}],"m1-lecture03-p005":[{"text":"**Derivational morphology** is how new words get made out of existing parts. The parts are called **morphemes** (units that add meaning or grammatical information). A morpheme is not the same as a syllable or a single speech sound.","children":[]},{"text":"The word morphology can be taken apart into two morphemes. This is because it was built from two Greek parts. The first part is morph-, which means form. The second part is -ology, which here means something like the study of. Since the two parts are put together, morphology means \"the study of the forms of words\".","children":[]},{"text":"A unit in text should get a name built the same way as morpheme and phoneme. This is because these unit names are built from Greek parts. The part morph- gives **morpheme**. Phone, another Greek morpheme that probably means speech, gives **phoneme**.","children":[]},{"text":"Graphology is where the pattern goes wrong. It looks like it should mean the study of units in text, but it does not mean that.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p006":[{"text":"The meaning of a new derived word can only be partly guessed from the word it came from. This is because **derivational morphology** is **productive** (it can make new words) only in limited ways. People who hear a made-up word often cannot guess what it means. So the meaning of the final form is only **partially predictable**.","children":[]},{"text":"To knife somebody, a person would use a weapon-like knife such as the prison shiv. This is because of how **zero derivation** (changing the part of speech without adding anything) works. It usually uses only a **narrow sense** of the base word. The noun knife covers many objects. An image search for knife gives different cutting tools and tableware. A search for prison shiv gives an improvised weapon. The verb knife covers only one kind of action, which is attacking somebody with a knife. Since the verb is that narrow, a table knife is a knife but does not fit the verb well.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p007":[{"text":"The suffix **-hood** can make new words, but it does not work on every word. This is because **derivational morphology** is limited in how widely and how regularly **affixes** (parts added to a stem) get used.","children":[]},{"text":"The -hood pattern takes a stem and adds -hood. The new word means a state of being that goes with the stem noun.","children":["Brotherhood and sisterhood follow the pattern.","Knighthood follows it in an odd way. Two brothers share their brotherhood, but each knight has his own knighthood."]},{"text":"Three words do not follow the pattern.","children":["Neighborhood is a perfectly good word, but -hood does a different job in it entirely. It does not mean the state of being a neighbor.","Friendhood is not what English says. English says friendship instead, for no apparent reason.","Enemyhood is not a word, though maybe it ought to be. The only words for the idea are less common ones such as enmity or antagonism."]},{"text":"Since -hood works for some stems and not others, each word has to be learned.","children":["Some of these words come from Liberman and Prince's introductory linguistics course at Penn."]}],"m1-lecture03-p008":[{"text":"No, the meanings of these words are not similar as verbs. The words are dish, spoon, fork and table, which are strong associates of the noun knife. This is because **zero derivation** (changing a word's category without adding a visible affix) does complicated things with **semantics** (meaning). The word looks the same, but its meaning as a verb is learned separately.","children":[]},{"text":"Each sentence uses a meaning that people have to learn.","children":["In \"She can dish it out but she can’t take it\", dish is a verb. Its meaning does not come from naming the object.","In \"He was born with a silver spoon and a forked tongue in his mouth\", forked uses the shape of a fork. Forked tongue also has an established figurative meaning.","In \"They decided to table the issue after a day of inconclusive negotiations\", table is a verb. Its meaning does not come from the furniture."]},{"text":"Since the meanings come from **conventional usage**, reading the words as utensils or furniture gets the sentences wrong.","children":["The silver spoon expression is the kind of thing that **Cognitive Linguistics** studies."]}],"m1-lecture03-p009":[{"text":"Derivation usually changes how a word sounds and how it is spelled. This is because **derivational morphology** often adds **affixes**. An affix can change the **phonology** (the sound pattern) of the rest of the word.","children":[]},{"text":"The certificate words make this concrete.","children":["The word certificate is pronounced /sər.ˈtI.fɨ.kɨt/.","The word certification is pronounced /ˌsər.tɨ.fɨ.ˈkeI.ʃən/.","The word recertification is pronounced /ˈri.sər.tɨ.fɨ.ˌkeI.ʃən/.","The **stress** lands on different syllables in the three words, and some vowels change too. So the base word does not keep its old pronunciation when an affix is added."]},{"text":"This is one reason for \"The Chaos\" of English spelling. Spelling that stays closer to the spoken form does not necessarily make words easier to read.","children":[]},{"text":"Some nouns and verbs are told apart mostly by **stress**.","children":["Record has one stress as a noun and another as a verb.","Invite does the same in \"He did not receive an invite\" and \"We did not invite him\".","Entrance also has two stress patterns, but those two words are not actually related to each other."]}],"m1-lecture03-p010":[{"text":"**Inflectional morphology** changes a word's meaning in a more predictable way than derivation does. This is because inflection only gives a different grammatical form of the same word. It does not make a new word. Menn puts it succinctly when she says “...we’d think it was silly to have separate dictionary entries for the singular and plural forms of nouns.”","children":[]},{"text":"English has three inflections here.","children":["The **plural** turns knife(n) into knives, one fork into two forks, and one mouse into a lot of mice.","The **third person singular present** turns knife(v) into knifes, I walk into she walks, and you run into he runs.","The **past** turns knife(v) into knifed. Today I walk becomes yesterday I walked. Today she sings becomes in the past, she sang."]},{"text":"The meaning is predictable, but the sound is not always predictable. This is because the phonological process of inflecting words often has some **irregularity**. Forks and walked just add an ending. Mice and sang change the word itself. Since mice is still plural and sang is still past, the grammar is the same either way.","children":["This irregularity comes up again with language learning models and kinds of developmental language impairment."]}],"m1-lecture03-p011":[{"text":"The rest of the sentence can force a word to be inflected. This is because **inflectional morphology** depends on **syntax** (how words are put together into phrases and sentences). In the phrase \"two [noun]+pl\", whatever noun goes in the slot must be made plural somehow. A regular noun gives two forks. An irregular noun gives two knives. Since the word two is there, the noun cannot stay singular.","children":[]},{"text":"Nothing in a sentence forces a word to be derived. This is because there are no syntactic processes like this for **derivational morphology**, even for very productive forms. The closest thing is a formula like “Once you [verb] it, you can un-[verb] it.” That formula is not syntax. No sentence makes a verb take an un- form.","children":[]},{"text":"Since only inflection is forced by the sentence, this is one way to tell inflection and derivation apart.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p012":[{"text":"English does not use **inflection** (changing a word's form for grammar) very much. This is because English only inflects for a few things.","children":["It marks **plurals**, as in knives, two forks and a lot of mice.","It marks the **third person singular present**, as in knifes, she walks and he runs.","It marks the **past**, as in knifed, walked and sang."]},{"text":"Many other languages do much more with inflection.","children":["Many languages conjugate verbs much more elaborately than English for **person**. They also do more for **tense**.","Many languages have different ways of making the **plural** for small numbers and for large numbers.","Many languages have **case markings** on nouns (markings that tell the noun's syntactic or semantic role)."]},{"text":"Since English leaves most of this out, English is a small case of what inflection can do.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p013":[{"text":"Derivation makes new words, and inflection changes a word to fit its sentence.","children":[]},{"text":"The two kinds of morphology are alike in two ways.","children":["Both make new phonological forms out of old parts.","Both change the meanings of base forms."]},{"text":"**Derivational morphology** creates **new words**. It is productive, but not always, and it is typically limited. This is because a pattern such as -hood works on some words and not on others.","children":[]},{"text":"**Inflectional morphology** changes how words interact syntactically with other words. It is **highly productive**, but it is not always regular. It is also **required by syntax**. This is because a phrase such as \"two [noun]+pl\" forces the plural, and some plurals such as mice are irregular.","children":[]},{"text":"To figure out which kind a change is, ask what the change does. The verb knife made from the noun knife is a new word, so it is derivation. The form knifes after he is the form the sentence needs, so it is inflection.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p014":[{"text":"A sentence is more than a list of single words. This is because words are grouped into **phrases**, **clauses** and **sentences**.","children":[]},{"text":"There are two kinds of words in these groups.","children":["**Content words** include nouns and verbs. They also include modifiers such as adjectives and adverbs.","**Function words** come in as soon as more than a couple of words are put together. Determiners and prepositions are function words. They tell how the content words go together."]},{"text":"The sentence “The video for “More than Words” came out before most of you were born” has two **noun phrases**.","children":["The first noun phrase is The video for “More than Words”.","The second noun phrase is most of you."]},{"text":"Since one noun phrase is long and the other is short, a noun phrase is not a set number of words. It is a group of words that works as one unit.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p015":[{"text":"The video for “More than Words” is a **noun phrase** with the structure **Det + N + PP**. This is because it has a determiner, then a noun, then a prepositional phrase.","children":[]},{"text":"Each part does one job.","children":["The is the **determiner** (Det). It is a function word, and it begins the noun phrase.","Video is the noun (N). It is the head of the phrase.","For is a **preposition**. It is a function word, and it begins the **prepositional phrase** (PP).","The prepositional phrase is what lets the song title tell which video is meant."]},{"text":"The title “More than Words” is not really part of the sentence. This is because it is being used like a noun, as the name of a song. If the quotes are taken away, it is not a phrase. It is just a bunch of words strung together in a row.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p016":[{"text":"Phrases with very different words can have the same structure. This is because the structure **Det + N + PP** is incredibly **abstract**. It names the kinds of parts and their order, not the words.","children":[]},{"text":"Three phrases have this structure.","children":["In \"two guys from the early ‘nineties\", two comes first, then guys, then from the early ‘nineties.","In \"a band with only one hit\", a comes first, then band, then with only one hit.","In \"one and only one phrase unrelated to early ‘nineties one-hit wonder Extreme\", one and only one comes first. Then comes phrase, then everything after it."]},{"text":"The parts are not the same size in each phrase.","children":["The **determiner** can be one word or several, as in one and only one.","The **head nouns** are guys, band and phrase.","The first two modifiers are **prepositional phrases** that start with from and with.","The third modifier starts with the adjective unrelated and then has a to phrase. So it is an adjective phrase with a prepositional phrase inside it."]}],"m1-lecture03-p017":[{"text":"This level of **abstraction** is needed to say that different phrases are the same kind of thing. This is because phrases can look very different on the surface. Abstraction leaves out the individual words. Without it, there is no way to talk about very different surface forms as similar.","children":[]},{"text":"The abstraction is also needed to compare languages. This is because all languages have **NPs** (noun phrases), but they can build them very differently.","children":["English says the cat in the coffee shop. The determiner comes first, and the PP comes after the noun.","Chinese says 咖啡店裡面的小貓, which is coffeeshop inside + cat. The order is **PP + N**, so the PP comes before the noun.","The Chinese NP has **no determiner**."]},{"text":"Since Chinese NPs have no determiner, this helps explain why speakers of Chinese languages find determiners very hard to learn in a second language (L2).","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p018":[{"text":"All of these **prepositional phrases** have the same structure, which is **P + NP**. This is because each one is a preposition (P) followed by a noun phrase (NP).","children":[]},{"text":"Taking three of them apart makes this clear.","children":["From the early ‘nineties is from and then the NP the early ‘nineties. That NP is Det + Adj + N.","With only one hit is with and then the NP only one hit. That NP is given as P + Det + N.","Unrelated to early ‘nineties one-hit wonder Extreme is unrelated to and then an NP. That NP is given as Adv + Adj + Adv + Adj + N."]},{"text":"The NP after the preposition can be short or long. Since the preposition connects that whole NP to the rest, the NP counts as one unit.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p019":[{"text":"Phrases are **hierarchical** (smaller phrases are inside bigger phrases). This is why a flat row of words with curly brackets and parentheses is a clumsy way to write them.","children":[]},{"text":"The phrase from the early ‘nineties has levels. This is because its parts are inside each other.","children":["The determiner, the adjective and the noun are all inside the **noun phrase**.","The noun phrase is inside the **prepositional phrase**.","The prepositional phrase is going to be inside a sentence, or inside yet another noun phrase."]},{"text":"Since phrases are inside other phrases, people started using **trees** to diagram sentences. A tree puts each phrase above the parts that are inside it. So a tree can keep every level apart, and a flat row cannot.","children":[]},{"text":"Hierarchy is also an important feature of languages.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p020":[{"text":"A **verb phrase** is a verb plus the phrases that go with it. Different verbs have different phrases after them.","children":[]},{"text":"Came out before most of you were born is **V + PP**.","children":["The verb is came out.","The PP is before most of you were born.","This PP is hard to take apart. This is because most of you were born looks like a verb phrase. It is there to give a point in time that complements before."]},{"text":"Many verb phrases also have **noun phrases** in them.","children":["Sang the song is **V + NP**. The NP is the song.","Worked himself to death is **V + NP + PP**. The NP is himself, and the PP is to death.","Threw a party for him is **V + NP + PP**. The NP is a party, and the PP is for him."]},{"text":"Since these phrases all belong with the verb, the verb alone is not the whole verb phrase.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p021":[{"text":"The whole sentence “The video for “More than Words” came out before most of you were born” is one **clause**. This is because a clause is a verb phrase (VP) and its **subject**. The subject is the noun phrase that controls the verb. This sentence has one VP and one subject, so it is one clause.","children":[]},{"text":"The sentence has two parts.","children":["The NP is The video for “More than Words”. It goes Det + N + PP.","The VP is came out before most of you were born. It goes V + PP."]},{"text":"The subject is the whole first NP. The **head noun** of that NP is video. The song title is inside the PP, so the title is not the subject.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p022":[{"text":"A verb agrees with the head noun of its own subject. That is what it means for the subject to **control** the verb.","children":[]},{"text":"In “The plums that were in the icebox were delicious”, the verb agrees with plums, not icebox. This is because plums is the **head** of the noun phrase. This sentence is one simple clause. Its parts are given as Det + N + PP and then VP.","children":[]},{"text":"If you changes to my roommate, the verb were probably saving changes to was probably saving. This is because “The plums in the icebox, which you were probably saving for breakfast, were delicious” has a **clause within a clause**. The inner clause is set off with commas.","children":["You is the subject of were probably saving for breakfast.","Plums is the subject of were delicious.","Since my roommate would only replace you, only the inner verb changes. Were delicious stays the same because its subject is still plums."]},{"text":"\"Forgive me\" has an **implicit subject** (a subject that is not said out loud). The speaker wants whoever shares his icebox to forgive him for eating the plums.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p023":[{"text":"Two sentences can mean the same thing and still have different subjects. This is because the **subject** is decided by the syntax, not by who did the action.","children":[]},{"text":"In \"I have eaten the plums in the icebox\", the subject is I. This is the **active** sentence.","children":["The subject comes first, and a verb phrase comes after it.","The verb phrase includes the NP the plums in the icebox. That NP is the **object** of the verb."]},{"text":"In \"The plums in the icebox were eaten by me\", the subject is the plums in the icebox. This is the **passive** sentence.","children":["The way to tell is agreement. The verb were eaten agrees with the plural noun plums.","By me is just a PP that fills out the VP. It tells who did the eating, but it is not the subject."]},{"text":"Since the same plums are the object in one sentence and the subject in the other, the two sentences are very different syntactically.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p024":[{"text":"The **semantic roles** do not change between the active and the passive sentence. This is because semantic roles are about what each participant does in the event. The event is the same in both sentences. The eater is the **agent**, the plums are the **undergoer**, and the icebox is the **location**.","children":[]},{"text":"Only the order and the **syntactic roles** (such as subject and object) change.","children":["The passive sentence goes Undergoer + Location + Verb + Agent. The plums is the undergoer, in the icebox is the location, and by me is the agent. Its subject is the undergoer.","The active sentence goes Agent + Verb + Undergoer + Location. I is the agent, the plums is the undergoer, and in the icebox is the location. Its subject is the agent."]},{"text":"It can seem counterintuitive that me is not the subject of the passive sentence. This is because English usually lines up the syntactic subject with the agent. Grade school teaches that grammar tells \"who did what to whom\". Semantic roles do that job more than syntactic roles do.","children":[]},{"text":"How the two kinds of roles match up also affects sentence processing.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p025":[{"text":"Language is highly **generative** (people can keep making new words and sentences out of familiar parts). This is because three things work together.","children":["**Derivational morphology** lets people make new words.","**Syntax** lets people organize words into clauses, clauses into phrases, and phrases into sentences.","**Inflectional morphology** lets people modify words for different syntactic roles."]},{"text":"People do this mostly without thinking explicitly about it. A speaker can make a plural or build a phrase without naming any grammar category.","children":[]},{"text":"This makes linguistics hard in one way. One of the hardest things to understand is what there is to explain. This is because some of the most complex phenomena are hiding in plain sight.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p026":[],"m1-lecture03-p027":[],"m1-lecture04-p001":[],"m1-lecture04-p002":[{"text":"**Association norms** (lists of the words people give for a cue word) come from asking people for the first words they think of. A person sees a word on a screen for a few seconds. Then the person writes down the first few words they think of.","children":[]},{"text":"This works because it asks for **immediate associations**, not a dictionary definition. Different people can write different words for the same cue. When many people do the task, you can count which answers come up again and again. You can also see which answers are unusual. Since the answers are collected from many people, the norm is a pattern of responses for each cue.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p003":[{"text":"LION is a **cue** (the word a participant responds to), and the participant comes up with whatever words it brings to mind. This is because the task records the person's own answer. The cue does not give the answer.","children":[]},{"text":"A person might write TIGER. That answer would connect two animals. But TIGER is just one possible answer, and nobody has to give it.","children":[]},{"text":"The answers tell you about the person's **lexical knowledge** (what they know about words and how words connect). Since everyone's answers can differ, you need many people to build a norm.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p004":[{"text":"TRIPE looks a lot like STRIPES, but the two words do not mean anything alike. This is because looking alike and meaning something alike are two different relationships.","children":[]},{"text":"TRIPE and STRIPES share a run of letters. So their **form** (spelling and sound) overlaps. But their **meanings** are not connected. Words with very different meanings can still share letters.","children":[]},{"text":"This makes the **arbitrariness** of form and meaning easy to see (the spelling of a word does not tell you what it means). Since form and meaning are separate, a shared spelling is not evidence of a shared meaning.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p005":[{"text":"FREE is a cue, and the participant comes up with their own words for it. This is because the task does not offer a list to choose from. The person just writes the words that come to mind.","children":[]},{"text":"The response is **generated** (made up by the person, not picked from options). Writing it down turns a hidden association into something you can look at. Then you can compare the answers across people.","children":[]},{"text":"Since each person makes up their own answer, the norm for FREE shows what people really connect with that word.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p006":[{"text":"STRIPES names a **visual property** (a feature you can see, here a pattern), so its associates can be a different kind of thing. This is because an association can link a property to an animal that has it.","children":[]},{"text":"STRIPES and TIGER can be associated. STRIPES is a pattern. TIGER is an animal. So the two words name different kinds of things but still go together.","children":[]},{"text":"This is not the same as **similarity** (sharing properties). LION and TIGER are similar because both are animals. STRIPES and TIGER are associated but not similar. Since these are different links, associated words do not have to name the same kind of thing.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p007":[{"text":"PEAR and BEAR look alike, but they name totally different things. This is because looking alike is a **formal relationship** (shared sound or spelling). Meaning alike is a **semantic relationship** (shared meaning).","children":[]},{"text":"PEAR is a fruit. BEAR is an animal. Their forms overlap, but that does not make them the same concept.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the two relationships are separate, a shared sound or spelling pattern is not evidence that two words mean similar things.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p008":[{"text":"BEAR is another cue that looks like PEAR but does not mean anything like it. This is because BEAR and PEAR overlap in form, not in meaning.","children":[]},{"text":"**Association norms** record the answers people actually give. The experimenter does not decide ahead of time whether an answer will come from form or from meaning. So whatever link the cue brings to mind gets recorded.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the norm just records real answers, it can catch links based on meaning and links based on form.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p009":[{"text":"TREE is a cue, and its norm adds up the different words that different people give for it. This is because the same word can bring different associates to mind for different people.","children":[]},{"text":"The first answers people give show which words are easily connected with TREE. That evidence comes from this one task.","children":[]},{"text":"The **norm** (the summary of everyone's answers) does not pick one answer as the only association. Since the cue stays the same word while the answers vary, the norm keeps all of them.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p010":[{"text":"TIGER connects to LION in one way and to STRIPES in a different way. This is because TIGER and LION are both animals, so they have **similar meanings**. TIGER and STRIPES go together because stripes are a **characteristic pattern** of tigers.","children":[]},{"text":"So an animal can be associated with one of its own properties. Two animal names can also be related because they are in the same category. Both kinds of link can be part of a **lexical network** (a network of connected words).","children":[]},{"text":"Since these two links are different, **association** and **similarity** are not the same thing.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p011":[],"m1-lecture04-p012":[{"text":"Only LION, TIGER and STRIPES are tabulated, because these three matter later. This is because they connect meanings in two different ways. LION and TIGER are **similar animals**. TIGER and STRIPES link an animal to a **characteristic property**. You can measure each association by counting how often people give one word when they see the other.","children":[]},{"text":"The pairs TRIPE and STRIPES, and PEAR and BEAR, make a different point. The words in each pair overlap in form. But similar form does not mean similar meaning. The link between how a word sounds or is spelled and what it means is largely **arbitrary** (not predictable).","children":[]},{"text":"Since these links can differ, keep three things apart. These are similar form, similar meaning and one word bringing another to mind.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p013":[{"text":"\"Associated\" is different from \"similar.\" **Association** is when one item tends to bring another to mind. **Similarity** is when two things share properties. TIGER and STRIPES are associated, but they are not two similar objects.","children":[]},{"text":"Did people have a particular strategy? They may have used a deliberate **strategy** to come up with words. That strategy can change which words they write.","children":[]},{"text":"Could the **list structure** have influenced the answers? Yes, the list structure could change them. This is because the other words on the list change which ideas are easy to think of. A list of only wild animals could push people toward one set of answers. A list of only household appliances could push them toward a different set.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the answers depend on the task, a norm only tells you about responses under those conditions.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p014":[{"text":"Menn's chapter is called \"How Brains Work,\" but a better title would be \"Some basic metaphors from neuroscience that are useful in psycholinguistics\". This is because the chapter gives a few ideas borrowed from neuroscience. These ideas are the basis for most theory and experimental design in the field.","children":[]},{"text":"**Activation** is how active a representation is.","children":[]},{"text":"**Spreading activation** is activity passing to connected representations over **weighted connections** (links of different strengths).","children":[]},{"text":"**Distributed representations** store an item as a pattern spread over many units.","children":[]},{"text":"**Associative learning**, such as **Hebbian learning**, makes links stronger between units that are active together.","children":[]},{"text":"**Bottom-up processing** goes from sensory features up to words. **Top-down processing** goes from word knowledge back down to the features.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p015":[{"text":"One brain cell can fire hardest for one exact combination of line angle and movement. **Hubel and Wiesel (1968)** found this when they related cell activity to sensory input. Their work happened at the same time as the **Cognitive Revolution**.","children":[]},{"text":"They recorded from **simple cells** in **primary visual cortex (V1)**. A simple cell responds to lines at certain angles inside its **receptive field** (the part of the visual input that the cell responds to).","children":[]},{"text":"They moved a line at different angles and compared how much the cell fired. The cell fired the most when a line at about **315 degrees** moved upward. It fired much less for other angles and movements.","children":[]},{"text":"This matters because a cell's **electrochemical activity** tells you what it represents and computes. Since this cell picks out one combination of features, you can say it **represents** that combination.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p016":[{"text":"Cells further along the **visual processing stream** respond to more complex things, like another monkey's face in profile. This is because, in data adapted from **Gross and colleagues (1985)**, one cell in **inferotemporal cortex** fires strongly for near-profile views of a monkey's face. It fires much less for front and back views. Its firing is highest around 80 to 100 degrees of face angle.","children":[]},{"text":"So it makes sense to say this cell **represents** something complex, not just line segments. Its activation is **graded** (it can be a little active or very active). It goes up a little for one view and a lot for another.","children":[]},{"text":"In psycholinguistics, **activation** borrows this idea as a metaphor. It applies it to **perceptual and conceptual representations**. Since activation is graded, a word or concept can also be more or less active.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p017":[{"text":"A very simple model treats each word as a **node** (a unit with an activation level) that the word can switch on. This is because imagining simple **lexical representations** (stored representations of words) makes the network easy to follow.","children":[]},{"text":"The network here has three nodes, for lion, tiger and stripes. A specific word activates its own node.","children":[]},{"text":"The printed word and the node are two different levels. The printed LION is the evidence you see. The lion node is the word the system could recognize from that evidence. Since the node has an activation level, the model can say how much activity a word gets.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p018":[{"text":"One word node has to respond to many different-looking versions of the same word. This is because LION can be printed as LION, lion or Lion. It can also come in different typefaces and sizes. Each version looks different on the screen. But all of them should switch on the same lion node.","children":[]},{"text":"This is the **lack of invariance problem** (one identity shows up in many different physical forms). The same problem came up with **phonemes**. To handle it, the **receptive fields** of word nodes would eventually have to be very complicated.","children":[]},{"text":"For now, just imagine there are nodes that words can activate. Since every version should reach the same node, the node maps many printed forms onto one word.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p019":[{"text":"A node can be only partly switched on. This is because the word LION here is inside a **noise mask** (visual noise around the word). LION does not stand out much from the noise. So the input gives only weak evidence for the word. So the lion node gets only **weak activation**.","children":[]},{"text":"The node can be partly active even while you still cannot tell what the word is. As more of the word comes through the noise, the activation can go up.","children":[]},{"text":"Since activation can be partial, a model can describe the stages before a word is identified.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p020":[{"text":"As the word gets clearer, the activation of its node goes up. This is because two things change together. On the screen, LION gets easier to tell apart from the noise. In the model, the lion node becomes **more active**.","children":[]},{"text":"Stronger evidence supports the same word more strongly. So recognizing a word can be modeled as a response that grows as the input gets stronger.","children":[]},{"text":"Since clarity and activation go up together, changing the display can change how a word is processed.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p021":[{"text":"When the word is clear enough, the network counts it as **identified**. This is because the **signal-to-noise ratio (SNR)** (how strong the word is compared with the noise) becomes very good. Then the lion node is active enough to say the network recognized lion. Tiger and stripes are not identified.","children":[]},{"text":"This is how the **perceptual identification task** works. Participants push a button as soon as they can tell what the word is. The time it takes is what gets measured. Anything that makes the word easier to identify makes the button press come earlier.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the press happens when activation is high enough, the reaction time tells you about activation in the model.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p022":[{"text":"What factors might change how quickly you identify a word? There are two kinds. This is because the speed can depend on the word itself and on the setting.","children":[]},{"text":"**Features of the stimulus itself** are about the target word. One is how often you run into the word. Another is how similar it is to other words.","children":[]},{"text":"**Features of the experimental environment** are about what came before the word. One is whether you just saw that same word. Another is whether you just saw a similar or related word.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the two kinds are separate, you can test the same word in different settings. You can also compare different words in the same setting.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p023":[{"text":"Four things can change how quickly you identify a word. Two are about the word itself, and two are about the experiment.","children":[]},{"text":"About the word, **word frequency** (how often you run into a word) matters. A common word can be easier to activate. How similar a word is to other words also matters, through **neighborhood** relationships.","children":[]},{"text":"About the experiment, **repetition priming** (being faster after just seeing the same word) matters. Just seeing a similar or related word can also speed things up.","children":[]},{"text":"Frequency and repetition priming are easy to mix up. Frequency is about experience with a word built up over time. Repetition priming is about seeing the word a moment ago. A related word changes the setting without repeating the target. Since these differ, you have to ask what changed between conditions.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p024":[{"text":"Words you see more often are easier to activate, because experience tunes up their **activation dynamics** (how activation rises and falls over time). This is because most models of this kind assume activation **unfolds over time**. Experience **tunes up** that process. Words you see more often become more sensitive. So they get activated more easily. That is one way to explain **frequency effects**.","children":[]},{"text":"The **Interactive Activation model** of **Rumelhart and McClelland (1986)** simulates this kind of process. For the word CAVE, activation climbs above the competing words while the input supports it. Then it slowly drops.","children":[]},{"text":"Different computational models simulate frequency effects in different ways. Since experience changes the dynamics, frequency is tied to how a word gets processed.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p025":[{"text":"Seeing a word again very soon makes it faster to identify. This is because activation does not go up and down instantly. It may **drop more slowly than it rises**. So some activation is still there after the word is gone.","children":[]},{"text":"If the word shows up again while activation is still high, its **baseline** (starting activation level) is already higher than usual. So it takes less to activate it.","children":[]},{"text":"This is **repetition priming**, a basic effect in psychology. Since the leftover activation gives the word a head start, the second identification is faster.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p026":[{"text":"Two factors still need explaining. These are **neighborhood effects** (effects of how similar a word is to other words) and priming from a similar or related word. That priming can be **associative**, **semantic** or **phonological**.","children":[]},{"text":"They need something new in the network. This is because a single word node can only change its own activity. Repetition priming involves the same word twice. But these two factors involve one word changing a **different** word.","children":[]},{"text":"Since one word has to affect another, the nodes need to be linked.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p027":[{"text":"To explain many things about language, the nodes in a network have to be **connected**. After all, it is a network.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because activity needs a way to get from one word to another. Lion is connected to tiger. Tiger is connected to stripes.","children":[]},{"text":"The printed word LION activates lion **directly**. Then the connection carries some activity from lion to tiger. That activity on tiger is **indirect**, since it came through the network and not from the screen.","children":[]},{"text":"Since nodes are connected, one active node can change how another node responds.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p028":[{"text":"Activating one word sends some activation to words it is associated with. This is called **spreading activation**.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because the connections can be set by the association norms. The **connection strength** between two nodes depends on how associated they are. When lion is activated, some activation travels along a **weighted connection** (a link with a strength) to tiger. Different links can pass different amounts.","children":[]},{"text":"This explains **semantic priming** (faster recognition of a word after a related word). Tiger is already partly active from lion. So tiger is easier to recognize when it shows up.","children":[]},{"text":"A semantic network can link many ideas. Vehicle links to car and bus. Fire engine links to the vehicles and to fire ideas. Since links spread activity, one active idea can boost several related ones.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p029":[],"m1-lecture04-p030":[{"text":"STRIPES starts out almost invisible inside a strong **noise mask**. This is the first step of a **perceptual identification** sequence. The contrast is very low. So the letters are hard to pick out from the background.","children":[]},{"text":"The word can already start to get some support from the input. But the evidence is too weak to say for sure what the word is. Since the evidence is so weak, nobody can identify it yet.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p031":[{"text":"As STRIPES gets more contrast, more of its letters stand out from the noise. This is because the letters, though still faint, are easier to pick out from the mask. So the input gives **stronger support** for the word.","children":[]},{"text":"The word itself did not change. Only the quality of the evidence changed. Since the word is the same, clarity changes how much evidence there is, not which word it is.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p032":[{"text":"STRIPES gets easier to identify as its contrast improves. This is because the letters now stand apart from the noise. So the evidence for the word keeps growing.","children":[]},{"text":"In the perceptual identification task, the person responds once there is enough evidence to recognize the word. Since better quality gives more evidence sooner, stimulus quality decides when the **identification response** can happen.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p033":[{"text":"The clearer STRIPES gives enough information for the participant to respond. This is because the letters are now clearer than in the heavily masked versions. The noise is the same kind as before.","children":[]},{"text":"A person who recognizes the word can now press the button. That press means they **recognized the word**. It is not a judgment about whether stripes go with some animal. Since the task only asks for recognition, it measures recognizing, not associating.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p034":[{"text":"At high contrast, STRIPES is easy to identify, and that switches on the **stripes node**. This is because the dark letters stand out clearly from the gray noise. Recognizing the word activates its node in the network.","children":[]},{"text":"Now STRIPES can act as a **prime** (an earlier word that may change how a later word is processed). Whether it affects another word depends on the **connections** between their nodes. Since the stripes node is now active, its activity can travel along those connections.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p035":[{"text":"LION is the **target** (the word being identified) in a second sequence, and it starts out hard to read. This is because LION has weak contrast against the noise mask. So its letters give little useful information.","children":[]},{"text":"The target can be kept separate from what came just before it. A **priming** account asks whether that earlier experience changes how fast LION is recognized. Since STRIPES came first, the question is whether it speeds up LION.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p036":[{"text":"As LION gets more contrast, the evidence for the word gets stronger. This is because LION is now faintly visible in the center of the mask. The letters start to separate from the noise.","children":[]},{"text":"So the same word causes a **changing internal response**. The word stays the same, but the quality of the evidence changes. Since the evidence keeps improving, recognizing LION gets more likely.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p037":[{"text":"What gets measured is **when** LION is identified. This is because LION is now clearer, and the four letters have more contrast than before. A response can happen once the evidence is enough to recognize it.","children":[]},{"text":"A priming comparison asks whether recognition happens **sooner** after one earlier word than after another. LION is the same target in both cases. Since only the earlier word differs, a faster time means priming.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p038":[{"text":"LION is now readable, and two things help its node get active. This is because the clear stimulus gives strong evidence for lion. That raises the activation of the lion node.","children":[]},{"text":"The first thing is the **clarity** of the word itself. The second thing is any **leftover activation from the context**. Since both add up, recognition starts from a state built by the input and the context together.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p039":[{"text":"The clear LION finishes the sequence, and its node can be activated in two ways. This is because the printed word activates the lion node **directly**. Other nodes can also affect it **through connections**.","children":[]},{"text":"The strong word signal is easy to tell apart from the noise. Since the lion node can get help through connections, the experiment asks whether STRIPES helped LION that way.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p040":[{"text":"Activating stripes can **prime lion**, even though the two words are **not directly associated**. This is because activity flows throughout the network. Stripes is connected to tiger. Tiger is connected to lion. So activation goes from stripes to tiger and then from tiger to lion.","children":[]},{"text":"This is called **mediated priming** (priming that passes through a middle word, here tiger). A model with only direct links has no path from stripes to lion. So that model could not explain the effect.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the effect needs activation to travel through tiger, it is **compelling evidence for spreading activation**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p041":[{"text":"The stripes and lion study is very typical of psycholinguistics. This is because it has **careful study design** and it finds **small effects**.","children":[]},{"text":"Why would stripes prime lion? Stripes primes lion because activity travels from stripes through tiger to lion. This is **counterintuitive** because stripes and lion are not obvious associates. A model with only direct links cannot explain the effect.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the two models predict different things, even a small effect can tell them apart.","children":[]},{"text":"Three broader issues remain. These are top-down and bottom-up processing, learning and distributed representations.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p042":[{"text":"Knowing a word can help you see a letter that is only partly visible. This is called **top-down processing** (an active word sends activation back down to its letters). It works together with **bottom-up processing** (information goes from visual features up to letters and then words).","children":[]},{"text":"The **Interactive Activation network** after **McClelland and Rumelhart (1981)** has three levels. Features are at the bottom. Letters are in the middle. Words such as WORK, WORD, WEAR and WEAK are at the top.","children":[]},{"text":"In the example, blots cover part of the last letter of WORK. The visible features fit more than one letter. But WORK becomes active and sends activation back down to K. So the output for **K goes up a lot** over time, while R and D stay low.","children":[]},{"text":"This is one way of modeling **pattern completion**, which Menn discusses at length.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p043":[{"text":"Knowledge gets into the brain through **associative learning**. This is because connections get stronger when units are active together. **Hebbian learning** sums this up as \"neurons that fire together, wire together.\" The real process is more complicated. But the neural learning mechanisms that are fairly well understood are **associative**.","children":[]},{"text":"In the bell and food example, a bell signal and a food signal both feed into a salivation unit. The bell and the food are active at the same time. So the **synapse** (connection) from the bell gets stronger. Then the bell has more effect.","children":[]},{"text":"People used to think these mechanisms were **not enough** to learn anything important about language. Now the debate is about what role they play and how **innate knowledge** might limit them. **Large language models (LLMs)** learn many structural features of language with little more than this.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p044":[{"text":"A model can generalize what it learns to new, similar things by using **distributed representations** (each item stored as a pattern spread over many units). This is because **generativity** is very important for language. To generalize, an associative model has to represent **similarity**. Similar items get overlapping patterns. So learning about one item also changes the patterns used for similar items.","children":[]},{"text":"So what you know about robins and canaries can carry over to new birds as you learn about them.","children":[]},{"text":"The network takes in an **item** and a **relation**. For canary with the relation can, the outputs include grow, move, fly and sing. Hidden layers connect the item and relation to those properties.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p045":[{"text":"Five ideas borrowed from neuroscience give psycholinguistics a way to talk about word knowledge.","children":[]},{"text":"**Activation** is how active a representation is, and it changes as input comes in.","children":[]},{"text":"**Spreading activation** lets one representation affect another through connections.","children":[]},{"text":"**Top-down and bottom-up processing** tell you which direction influence goes between levels.","children":[]},{"text":"**Associative learning**, such as Hebbian learning, changes how strong connections are. **Distributed representations** let a model handle similarity and generalize.","children":[]},{"text":"**Priming experiments** help figure out what people's **lexical networks** look like. This is because they measure how processing one word changes processing of another.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p046":[],"m1-lecture04-p047":[],"m1-lecture05-p001":[],"m1-lecture05-p002":[{"text":"**Phrenology** tried to figure out what parts of the brain do by measuring the shape of people's skulls. A simplified phrenological chart marks head regions for language, memory, thinking, love and other things.","children":[]},{"text":"It rested on three premises.","children":["Different parts of the brain are responsible for different processes.","**Aptitude** (how good someone is at something) is related to the size of a brain region.","The size of a brain region changes the shape of the skull.","If all three were true, careful skull measurements would reveal brain functions."]},{"text":"No one believes in phrenology anymore, but not every premise is completely wrong. This is because the first premise is still a serious idea, since brain parts can do different jobs. The third premise is the clearly wrong one, because skull shape is not a sensitive measure of brain shape. The second premise would need a dependable link from an ability to region size before skulls could tell anything.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p003":[{"text":"Phrenology failed for two reasons, one about anatomy and one about psychology.","children":[]},{"text":"The first reason is **inappropriate anatomical measurement**. This is because skull shape is not a sensitive measure of brain shape. So measuring the head cannot tell you what the brain underneath looks like.","children":[]},{"text":"The second reason is an **unconstrained definition of functions** (no limits on what counted as a mental function). This is because researchers picked broad social labels and treated each one as a single brain function.","children":["Studies of prisoners tried to find the region for \"criminality\".","Studies of women with different numbers of children tried to find the spot for \"philopropogenitiveness\"."]},{"text":"Since both the measure and the functions were bad, phrenology could not connect a real brain part to a real mental process.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p004":[{"text":"**Flourens** was an early and vociferous critic of phrenology, and his lesion experiments argued against its map. He used **experimental lesions** (damaging or removing brain tissue and then watching how behavior changes). He worked mainly with pigeons and rabbits.","children":[]},{"text":"He did find that big brain parts do different things. This is because his experiments showed **distinct functions** for the cerebral cortex, the cerebellum and mid-brain structures.","children":[]},{"text":"But he **failed to find** specific cortical regions for specific senses. This is because senses did not drop out one at a time. When one sense was lost, all were lost together. For example, when most of the cortex is removed, sight, hearing and other senses disappear together.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the senses went together, he insisted that the **size of the lesion** was what mattered, not its location.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p005":[{"text":"In **1861**, the neurologist **Pierre Paul Broca** presented a patient called **\"Tan\"** who could not speak but seemed to understand. Tan had lost the ability to speak after a brain injury decades earlier. Broca gave this talk to the newly established Society of Anthropology, where he was also the founding president.","children":[]},{"text":"Tan's case mattered because his abilities came apart. This is because one ability was badly damaged while others looked fine.","children":["Tan could only produce **one jargon syllable**.","Otherwise he seemed to be \"in possession of his senses\".","He even seemed to **understand** what was being said."]},{"text":"This was not the first case of a specific loss of speech. Since Tan lost speaking but kept understanding, the case suggested that **producing speech** and **understanding speech** can be damaged separately.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p006":[{"text":"Tan's brain gave its name to **Broca's area**, a part of the **left frontal lobe** important for speech. Tan's brain is now on display at the Musee Dupuytren.","children":[]},{"text":"The name comes from where his damage was. This is because Tan had a **large lesion** (an area of damaged tissue). The lesion definitely included the **third convolution of the frontal lobe**, also called the **inferior frontal gyrus**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Hughlings Jackson (1874)** criticized this at the time. He wrote that \"to locate the damage which destroys speech and to locate speech are two different things\". This is because breaking one spot can stop speech even if speech depends on much more. Since damage only shows what stops working, it does not show where speech itself happens.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p007":[{"text":"In the **1870s**, German neurologists **Lichtheim** and **Wernicke** explained different language losses as broken links between brain **\"centers\"**. They made more systematic observations of **aphasia** (language loss after brain damage). A center is a proposed brain spot for one function.","children":[]},{"text":"They said a specific loss comes from a **\"disconnection\"** between two centers. In Lichtheim's house model, A is sound images, B is concepts and M is movement images.","children":["Losing the ability to understand spoken words might come from a break between \"A\" and \"B\". This is because understanding needs the heard sound to reach its meaning.","Losing verbatim repetition might come from a break between \"A\" and \"M\". This is because repeating needs the heard sound to reach the speech movements."]},{"text":"Describing centers for specific functions like this is an early form of **modularity**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p008":[{"text":"**Sigmund Freud** argued that the brain is probably not organized around our psychological categories. He critiqued the **\"diagram-drawing\" school** of aphasia research in his early neurological work.","children":[]},{"text":"He felt it was naive to expect brain areas for things like \"word forms\" or \"concepts\". This is because those are labels we use to describe behavior. Nothing guarantees the brain is built around those same labels.","children":[]},{"text":"He proposed instead that language comes from **\"fields\"** that connect perceptual and motor abilities to **\"drives\"**. This is a **holist critique** (the view that language is spread across connected systems instead of sitting in separate centers).","children":[]},{"text":"Freud's point leaves a **durable tension** in cognitive neuroscience. Can brain area functions be matched directly to cognitive functions? Is the brain really organized the way we describe cognition?","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p009":[{"text":"MRI showed that the damage in Broca's famous cases was much deeper than Broca's area. **Dronkers and colleagues** used **MRI** to measure the size and location of damage in Broca's landmark cases very precisely, including Leborgne's brain (Tan).","children":[]},{"text":"This is because the scans could see inside the brain, not just its surface.","children":["The lesion goes very deep and includes the **insula**.","There is also a lot of **white matter** damage to the **superior longitudinal fasciculus** (a tract that connects the front and back of the cortex)."]},{"text":"Other work by this group found that the **insula**, not Broca's area, is strongly tied to **speech apraxia**. Speech apraxia is the speaking problem Broca described in his patients. Since the deeper damage fits that problem, Broca's area may not be the main cause of Tan's lost speech.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p010":[{"text":"Broca is still talked about 150 years later, even though both his behavior description and his anatomy were crude.","children":[]},{"text":"His description of behavior was crude because of the time. This is because linguistics then focused on history, like how languages were related and reconstructing ancient languages. Experimental psychology had not really been invented yet. So observations of behavior had to be informal.","children":[]},{"text":"His anatomical localization was also crude, and probably wrong. This is because detailed descriptions of the cerebral cortex were still in their infancy. For example, **Brodmann's map** of cortical regions was not published until **1909**.","children":[]},{"text":"Since these tools did not exist yet, Broca's limits came from his time, not just from him.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p011":[{"text":"Broca's presentation of Tan is still discussed because it made linking **specific functions** to **specific brain regions** a **respectable pursuit**.","children":[]},{"text":"Other people found similar things around the same time. **The Daxes** reported related cases. **Wernicke** reported a patient who was **fluent** but produced **meaningless gibberish**. If anything, Wernicke's data are more interesting, because his patient talked easily but made no sense.","children":[]},{"text":"Still, for a variety of reasons, Broca's case is seen as a **watershed moment** in neuropsychology. This is because it convinced researchers that this kind of question was worth serious study.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p012":[],"m1-lecture05-p013":[{"text":"**Cognitive neuropsychology** (studying how brain damage changes mental abilities) needs **cognitive psychology** to say what the abilities are. Much of what was learned about brain structure and function in the 20th century came from testing people with brain damage.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because a theory says which mental operations might be separate. For example, is recognizing the **abstract form** of a word separate from figuring out its **meaning**? If damage hurts one but not the other, the two can be **dissociated** (pulled apart).","children":[]},{"text":"Tasks can target different levels.","children":["**Physical identity** matching asks if two letters look the same.","**Name identity** matching asks if two letters have the same name.","**Rule identity** matching asks if two letters are both vowels or both consonants."]},{"text":"**Quantitative measurements** let researchers compare abilities statistically. This matters because **deficits are rarely complete**, so a patient is usually worse at something, not totally unable.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p014":[{"text":"**Anatomical imaging** lets researchers see where a brain injury is while the patient is still alive. Before imaging, scientists had to wait for patients to \"come to autopsy\" to find the lesion. A scan of **H.M.'s** middle temporal lobes is one example, and his damage came from surgery.","children":[]},{"text":"But imaging the damage still does not tell what the healthy region does. This is because of the point from **Hughlings Jackson (1874)**. Locating the damage that destroys speech is different from locating speech.","children":[]},{"text":"Patient studies show what behavior looks like **after** a region is damaged. Since they only show the damaged brain, they tell only **indirectly** what the region does when intact.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p015":[{"text":"**PET** and **fMRI** let researchers watch brains in action, but only in an indirect, slow and blurry way. **Posner and colleagues** described PET studies that used radio-labeled oxygen to mark activity.","children":[]},{"text":"They are indirect because they are **metabolic**. This is because they measure an index of **oxygen consumption**, not the neurons' activity itself.","children":[]},{"text":"They are slow because metabolic processes take **seconds**, while important neural events can happen in **milliseconds** or less.","children":[]},{"text":"They are blurry because of their **fair spatial resolution**. FMRI is better than PET, but both see detail only down to **millimeters**. Important things happen at the scale of **microns**, which is much smaller.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p016":[{"text":"You cannot just say \"x part of the brain is active during y task\", so imaging studies need a **control condition**. This is because there is activity all over the brain all of the time. So seeing activity during a task tells you nothing on its own.","children":[]},{"text":"**Subtraction logic** fixes this. The researcher takes the task image and subtracts the control image. What is left is the activity for the part that differs between the two. For example, a motor image minus a rest image leaves a much smaller region.","children":[]},{"text":"**Posner and colleagues** used this to separate recognizing word and letter forms from processing word meaning. Their tasks shared the same visual input and responses. The key difference was whether **word meaning** was needed. Since meaning was the key difference, the leftover activity reflects **semantic processing**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p017":[{"text":"The region **Posner and colleagues** found for word meaning roughly matches **Broca's area**.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because three meaning tasks all lit up the same **left inferior frontal** region.","children":["Watching words for dangerous animals was compared with just viewing words.","Thinking of uses for written words was compared with repeating them.","Thinking of uses for heard words was compared with repeating them."]},{"text":"The same region appeared whether words were seen or heard. Since it appeared across all three meaning contrasts, it is linked to **semantic processing** (working with word meaning).","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p018":[{"text":"Is this better than phrenology? It can be, but only if the measurement and the definitions are good. The question is rhetorical but worth exploring.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because phrenology failed in two ways. It used a bad measure, skull shape. It used vague functions, like \"criminality\".","children":[]},{"text":"Imaging fixes both problems in part. It measures an index of **brain activity** instead of skull shape. It defines the operation with a **controlled task contrast** instead of a broad label.","children":[]},{"text":"A colorful image of an active region still proves nothing by itself. The contrast has to really isolate the operation. Since the image only shows what differs between tasks, the task design decides what it means.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p019":[{"text":"**Posner and colleagues** answered by localizing small **component operations**, not whole tasks like reading. These operations work on **visual**, **phonological** (sound) and **semantic** (meaning) codes.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because a whole task needs many computations working together. Reading a word, forming a visual image and shifting attention each use several of them. So no whole task belongs to one brain area.","children":[]},{"text":"Their PET evidence **complemented lesion evidence**, because it showed activity while people performed tasks. Averaged blood-flow patterns looked **consistent** across participants. The participants were right-handed and good readers.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p020":[{"text":"Language is good for studying how brain parts relate to functions because it splits into separate, testable abilities. Language has shown up at key moments in debates about **localization of function**.","children":["**Broca's presentation of Tan** helped start **lesion-behavior analysis** (comparing damaged brain areas with lost abilities).","Some of the first **PET** and **fMRI** studies used activation of **Broca's area** in language tasks as evidence that functions can be localized."]},{"text":"This is because language abilities can come apart. Tan lost speaking but seemed to keep understanding. Understanding words, repeating them and judging their meaning each need different processing. Since these abilities differ, researchers can build clear contrasts for patient studies and control conditions.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p021":[],"m1-lecture06-p001":[],"m1-lecture06-p002":[{"text":"Language can be described as a **formal symbolic system** (abstract symbols plus rules for combining them). This way of describing language is very old, and it uses terms like those in **Artificial Intelligence**.","children":[]},{"text":"In the 1950s and 1960s, experimental psychology was actively hostile to this idea. This is because it talks about an **internal state** inside the mind. Behaviorists only wanted to explain learning with things they could observe. Those things were stimuli, responses and consequences.","children":[]},{"text":"**Skinner's Verbal Behavior** tried to explain how language is learned by **operant conditioning** (learning because a response gets reinforced). **Chomsky's (1959) review** of that book argued it could not explain what speakers know. The review is better remembered than the book itself.","children":[]},{"text":"The review was an important moment in **the Cognitive Revolution**. This is because Chomsky treated a **grammar** as something inside the speaker. A grammar can handle sentences the person has never heard. Since that internal system had to be taken seriously, internal mental states came back into psychology.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p003":[{"text":"The formal tradition comes from early computers. Interest in **Artificial Intelligence** goes back to the beginning of the digital computer. **Alan Turing** thought about this a lot. He built decryption devices for the British Army during World War II.","children":[]},{"text":"Computers made people ask how a machine differs from **human intelligence**, if at all. This is because computers were invented as **universal function approximators** (machines that can do many kinds of calculations and logical operations). No one had built a machine that did so many different kinds of operations before. Earlier machines did just one fixed job.","children":[]},{"text":"Since one machine could run many different procedures, people could ask where the real difference with a human lies. It might be in the procedures, in the representations, or in something else. So language research got tied to AI.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p004":[{"text":"The **Turing test** says a computer is intelligent if it can take a player's place in a conversation game without anyone noticing.","children":[]},{"text":"The test is built on the **imitation game**, which has three players. A and B are a man and a woman. C is an examiner, and C's job is to figure out which is the man and which is the woman. A tries to fool C. B tries to help C guess the right answer.","children":[]},{"text":"In the test, a **computer** replaces either A or B. If C does not notice the swap, the computer has demonstrated **intelligence**. This is because C can only judge from the conversation. C never gets to look at the machine's program.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the whole judgment happens through talking, **natural language** is what the whole test runs on.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p005":[{"text":"The Turing test is about everyday language and learning, not about expert skills.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because the test runs on **natural language**. The machine has to understand questions and give answers that fit.","children":[]},{"text":"The test also involves **deception**. One player is trying to fool the examiner. So the machine has to cope with an examiner who may be trying to catch it.","children":[]},{"text":"Turing explicitly left out **expert behaviors**. Calculating large sums does not count. Composing poetry does not count either. Holding a normal conversation is a different job from one expert skill.","children":[]},{"text":"Turing concluded that the best way to get an intelligent machine is to start with a **child**. The machine would have the cognitive abilities of a child. It would then learn to be more like an **adult**. Since it learns, its intelligence comes from experience, not from a finished list of adult answers.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p006":[{"text":"**Newell and Simon's General Problem Solver** solved problems by moving symbols around with procedures. It is a later example of **GOFAI** (the approach that treats intelligence as working with explicit symbols).","children":[]},{"text":"This is because the program was given an abstract description of a formal problem. Each setup of the problem is a **state**. A legal move turns one state into another state. The program keeps applying procedures until it reaches the **goal state**.","children":[]},{"text":"It was very good at games and puzzles. In the **Tower of Hanoi**, each arrangement of disks is a state. Each legal move connects two arrangements. It also handled **chess**.","children":[]},{"text":"It was good at mathematics and logic too. It did **geometry proofs**. It did **algebra word problems**. Since all of these have clear states and rules, they suited the program well.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p007":[{"text":"Early AI focused on **formal problems** for two reasons. One reason was the technology, and the other was a theory about intelligence.","children":[]},{"text":"The first reason was **technology**. Inputs to computers were still very limited. People fed them punchcards and machine language. There was no way to give a computer a high-resolution image and say \"find the face\". So symbols were much easier to give a computer than images.","children":[]},{"text":"The second reason was a **theoretical commitment**. Researchers thought **rational behavior** like puzzle solving is what intelligence looks like. You can train a **pigeon** to guide a missile, but the pigeon does not know what it is doing. Whether AI programs know what they are doing is a deep argument too. Still, AI programs at least **represent goal states**. They also choose procedures for reaching them. Since programs do this, they looked closer to real intelligence than a trained animal.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p008":[{"text":"Chomsky said that knowing a language means having a **grammar** inside your head. Conditioning cannot explain it.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because a grammar lists sentences and gives each one a meaningful **structural description**. That lets a speaker do remarkable things. A speaker can tell **sentences from nonsentences**. A speaker can partly understand **new sentences**. A speaker can notice **ambiguities**.","children":[]},{"text":"New sentences are not linked to familiar ones by any simple **similarity**. They do not look alike in form, meaning or statistics. They do not share the same grammatical frame either. So Chomsky called talk of **generalization** here pointless and empty.","children":[]},{"text":"We recognize a new sentence because our internalized grammar **generates** it. We understand it partly by figuring out how the grammar **derives** it.","children":[]},{"text":"All normal children learn comparable, very complex grammars remarkably fast. Chomsky took this to mean humans are **specially designed** for language. He proposed a **hypothesis-formulating** ability of unknown character.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p009":[{"text":"Knowing a language means knowing a **grammar** (a set of abstract rules that produce grammatical sentences).","children":[]},{"text":"This matters because there are an **infinite** number of grammatical sentences. No one could ever hear them all. Yet people can make a **grammaticality judgment** (deciding whether a sentence is well formed) for any new sentence. That is remarkable, since the sentence was never heard before. So the judgment must come from the rules, not from memory.","children":[]},{"text":"The grammar also explains ambiguity. One string of words can get more than one structure. So knowing the words alone does not settle the meaning.","children":[]},{"text":"Because of this, people's **intuitions** about whether sentences are grammatical are the key data for studying language. Researchers ask which sentences people accept, not just which ones they say.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p010":[{"text":"Grammatical sentences do not look like each other in any obvious way. So the behaviorist idea of **stimulus generalization** cannot handle new sentences.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because stimulus generalization (a trained response spreading to things that look similar) needs similarity. A new item gets the response only if it resembles the trained item. Grammatical sentences have no obvious similarity to one another. A grammar can also generate an **infinite** number of them. Since there is no shared similarity, generalization has nothing to spread along.","children":[]},{"text":"Chomsky gave a different account. We recognize a new sentence because our **internalized grammar** generates it. The sentence does not need to match one we already know. Spreading a trained response to similar things is a different process from recognizing grammar.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p011":[{"text":"Conditioning spreads to new things only along concrete similarities like color. It is unclear how that could work for abstract ideas like grammar.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because of how **stimulus generalization** works in behaviorism. A **pigeon** is trained to peck for a **green light**. It then also pecks at similar colors. It pecks most near green and less as the color gets more different. This pattern is a **generalization gradient** (responding drops as the stimulus gets less similar).","children":[]},{"text":"Another pigeon does a **magnitude discrimination** task. It responds based on amounts, so number works like color here.","children":[]},{"text":"In both cases the similarity is concrete, such as **color** or **number**. Grammar has no concrete scale like that. Since there is no such scale, it is not clear how conditioning extends to **abstract concepts**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p012":[{"text":"**Stimulus control** is Skinner's idea that something in the environment controls what a person says. Chomsky argued it only works if you can name the stimulus apart from the response.","children":[]},{"text":"Skinner's typical example is someone responding to a piece of **music** with an utterance. The music is treated as the stimulus. The utterance is treated as the response.","children":[]},{"text":"Chomsky said other examples only add to the **general mystification**. Skinner even treated a **proper noun** as a response to a stimulus.","children":[]},{"text":"This is a problem because a piece of music has many properties. A speaker could react to any one of them. If we use the utterance to decide which property was the stimulus, the utterance picks its own cause. Since nothing is named ahead of time, nothing is really explained.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p013":[{"text":"Skinner was asking the **wrong question**, not just giving a bad answer.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because we cannot predict what someone will say from the stimuli around them. We do not know what the current stimuli are until the person responds. So naming the stimulus afterward makes the account **circular** (it uses the answer to explain itself).","children":[]},{"text":"What people say, and when, depends on a whole mix of things. It depends on **social interactions**. It depends on **cognitive processes**. It depends on **previous experiences**. Much of this has nothing to do with language.","children":[]},{"text":"So Chomsky proposed a **tractable problem** (one that can actually be solved). Researchers study the **structure** of what people say. From that structure, they try to work out the **rules** that produce it. Since the rules describe possible sentences, they do not have to explain why one person says one thing.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p014":[{"text":"A speaker can tell that a sentence is grammatical without ever having heard it, even when its meaning makes little literal sense.","children":[]},{"text":"Chomsky (1955) compared \"colorless green ideas sleep furiously\" with \"furiously sleep ideas green colorless\". Neither sentence, nor any of their parts, had ever come up in an English speaker's past experience. Still, the first is **grammatical**, while the second is **not grammatical**.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because the first one follows English structure. Adjectives come before the noun, and a verb and an adverb follow it. The second one uses the same words in reverse. That breaks the structure. Since the words are the same, the difference must be the **structure**.","children":[]},{"text":"The opposite case also happens. The warning \"Car! Getting towed! Yours!\" is **ungrammatical**. Yet the stimulus that probably controlled it is perfectly clear. So getting a message across and being grammatical are separate things.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p015":[{"text":"Chomsky describes language at the **computational level of analysis**. The term comes from **David Marr**, a visual neuroscientist.","children":[]},{"text":"Marr said every cognitive system can be described at three **levels of analysis**.","children":[]},{"text":"The **computational level** describes what problem the system is solving.","children":[]},{"text":"The **algorithmic level** describes the steps and representations used to solve it.","children":[]},{"text":"The **implementational level** describes the physical machinery that carries out those steps.","children":[]},{"text":"These levels ask different questions about the same ability. This is because knowing what counts as the right answer is different from knowing the steps. Both are different from knowing the hardware. Since Chomsky's grammar says what language must achieve, it sits at the computational level. An example makes this clearer.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p016":[{"text":"A **cash register** does the same job no matter what machine does it. This example borrows heavily from **Sharon Goldwater**.","children":[]},{"text":"At the **computational level**, the register takes item prices and figures out the amount due. Given the payment, it figures out the change.","children":[]},{"text":"At the **algorithmic level**, it uses **addition** for the price. It uses **subtraction** for the change.","children":[]},{"text":"At the **implementational level**, the machinery does not matter. This is because a credit card reader or a computer program also counts as a cash register. Each one does the same adding and subtracting. For the same prices and payment, each gives the same change. Since the answer never depends on the machine, the job can be described without the hardware.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p017":[{"text":"Language can be treated as a **Type I problem** (Marr's term for a problem that splits cleanly into his levels). Then researchers can study grammar without worrying about how it is implemented.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because the work divides into levels. The computational level for language is **Universal Grammar**. The algorithmic level is the specific processes and how they apply in different languages.","children":[]},{"text":"The implementational level gets set aside. That level includes the **errors** people make. It includes **memory limitations**. It includes **associative learning**. Since these are set aside, grammar can be studied as a system of its own.","children":[]},{"text":"The timing is easy to mix up. Chomsky's critique came about a decade before Marr's theoretical work. Both were influential in AI and in theoretical work in the **GOFAI** tradition.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p018":[{"text":"A **Type 2** problem is one where many processes work at the same time. Their interaction is its own simplest description, so it cannot be split into clean levels.","children":[]},{"text":"Marr first connected his idea to Chomsky. Chomsky's **competence theory** for English syntax is exactly what Marr meant by a computational theory. Both care little about the messy details of the algorithms. Finding good algorithms is still hard. But first you have to know what they are supposed to do. A problem that splits this way has a **Type 1 theory**.","children":[]},{"text":"Many problems in biological information processing have a Type 1 theory. There is no reason they all should, though. This is because some problems are solved by many processes acting at once. When that happens, describing the interaction is the simplest description there is. Since nothing simpler exists, the problem has a **Type 2 theory**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p019":[{"text":"Marr suggested that the grammar of natural language may have a **Type 2** theory rather than a Type 1 theory.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because of what language does. A speaker has a **data structure** in mind that is not one-dimensional. Language turns it into a one-dimensional **sequential utterance**. The listener then rebuilds a rough copy of the original in their head.","children":[]},{"text":"Seen this way, English syntax may have no Type 1 theory of the kind **transformational grammar** tries to define. The rules of syntax may just be **wired-in conventions** for doing this tedious but vital job. They may not be deep principles about intelligence.","children":[]},{"text":"So an abstract theory of syntax may be an **illusion**. It would only roughly match what the processes really do. Marr compared it to **Julesz's second order statistics theory**. That theory only roughly matches the processes behind texture vision.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p020":[{"text":"How language is implemented in the **brain** might be impossible to leave out of a theory of language.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because language may come from many processes working together. If it does, then learning, memory, perception and action shape the result. So the way the brain is organized may shape the way language is organized.","children":[]},{"text":"A whole alternative approach to language research is built on this hypothesis. The usual approach starts from an abstract grammar that the brain must carry out. The alternative starts from the learner and how the brain processes things. It asks what language-like patterns can grow out of that. Since the starting point differs, the two approaches explain language differently.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p021":[{"text":"The alternative approach is called **emergentism**, **statistical learning** or **parallel distributed processing**. Its strongest claims are about the **implementational level**.","children":[]},{"text":"The first claim is **distributed representations** (non-hierarchical patterns spread over many units). This is like activity patterns over groups of neurons.","children":[]},{"text":"The second claim is **gradual learning**. Learning slowly adjusts the strength of connections. This is like changing the strength of synaptic connections.","children":[]},{"text":"The third claim is a focus on **microstructure** (the many small interactions that add up to a bigger pattern). It also draws a more graded line between **levels of analysis**.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the learning system itself builds the patterns, the apparent rules of language are expected to come from it.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p022":[{"text":"**Elman (1990)** built **simple recurrent networks** to capture different parts of language performance. The model only tries to predict the next item it will see.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because it learns by guessing and correcting. The current **input** goes to the **hidden units**. The hidden units make an **output** guess about the next item. A copy of the hidden activity is saved in the **context units**. When the next input comes in, the context units feed back into the hidden units.","children":[]},{"text":"The learning connections slowly adjust to make better guesses. The copying connection stays fixed.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the context units hold on to what came before, the guess depends on the current item and the earlier ones. The model gradually gets better with experience.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p023":[{"text":"A **letter-prediction model** can find words even though the text has no spaces.","children":[]},{"text":"The model reads \"Manyyearsagoaboyandgirllivedbytheseatheyplayedhappily\". It ends up splitting it into \"many years ago a boy and girl lived by the sea they played happily\".","children":[]},{"text":"This is because the model only tries to guess the next letter. **Prediction error** (how wrong each guess is) changes as it reads. At the start of a word, many letters could come next, so the error is high. Inside a word, each letter narrows down what comes next. So the error goes down within words. At the next word, the error jumps back up.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the error jumps at each new word, the jumps mark the **word boundaries**. Nobody told the model where words were.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p024":[{"text":"A similar model trained on **simple sentences** starts to sort words into **grammatical classes**. It even picks up some **semantic information** (meaning).","children":[]},{"text":"This is because the categories come as a **side effect** of learning to predict. Knowing what kind of word just came helps guess what comes next. Words used in similar spots end up with similar **hidden-unit activation patterns**. Elman looked at these patterns and grouped the similar ones.","children":[]},{"text":"The groups follow grammar. **Nouns** end up in one group and **verbs** in another.","children":[]},{"text":"The groups also follow meaning. Nouns split into **animates** and **inanimates**. Animates split into humans and animals. Inanimates include **food** and **breakables**. Verbs split by how they take a **direct object**. Since nobody labeled the words, the model figured out these groups on its own.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p025":[{"text":"A statistical model can tell \"colorless green ideas sleep furiously\" apart from the reversed version. Many engineering tools in **Natural Language Processing** work this way.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because they analyze **massive corpora** (collections of texts and transcriptions of spoken language). They pick up statistical patterns from them.","children":[]},{"text":"**Peter Norvig** discussed Chomsky's critique. He agreed that neither sentence appeared in published writing before 1955. But every two-word part had appeared. \"Colorless green\" was in a 1942 book. \"Green ideas\" was in The New Republic in 1922. \"Ideas sleep in books\" was in Current Opinion in 1912.","children":[]},{"text":"**Pereira (2001)** used a **finite-state model** with word categories added. It was trained by **expectation maximization** on newspaper text. It figured that sentence (a) is **200,000 times more probable** than sentence (b). Since both use the same words, the model is responding to word order.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p026":[],"m1-lecture06-p027":[{"text":"Psycholinguistics has two main traditions. They disagree about what linguistic knowledge is.","children":[]},{"text":"The **GOFAI, modular or Chomskyan** tradition says linguistic knowledge is abstract and symbolic. It says linguistic representations are different in kind from other knowledge. This knowledge still has to **interface** (connect) with messy perception, movement, memory and other mental abilities. So the psycholinguist's job is to study that interface.","children":[]},{"text":"The **emergentist or functionalist** tradition says symbol and rule-like behavior are **emergent properties** of a statistical learning process. Emergent properties are patterns that come out of a process instead of being built in. It says perception, movement and thinking actually shape linguistic representations. So the psycholinguist's job is to study **language use**. That means connecting language use to more general psychological patterns.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p028":[],"m1-lecture06-p029":[],"m1-lecture06-p030":[{"text":"Language is messy at every level, just like the exceptions to the **\"i before e\" rule**. This messiness is why the **emergentist or statistical learning** approach exists.","children":[]},{"text":"**Phonetic categories** (speech sound categories) are defined by probabilities over several dimensions.","children":[]},{"text":"**Words** are ambiguous in every way imaginable.","children":[]},{"text":"**Morphological paradigms** have exceptions. English forms the past tense by adding -ed. But RUN-RAN and SLEEP-SLEPT break that pattern.","children":[]},{"text":"**Syntactic rules** change between dialects and individuals. They also change with innovation and over time.","children":[]},{"text":"Since no level follows one clean rule, a learning system has to handle both the main pattern and its exceptions.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p031":[{"text":"**Moravec's paradox** is a problem for the **GOFAI** approach. Tasks that are easy for computers are hard for humans, and the other way around.","children":[]},{"text":"For computers, proving theorems and solving geometry problems is fairly easy. Recognizing a face is extremely hard for them. Crossing a room without bumping into anything is extremely hard too.","children":[]},{"text":"For humans, it is the opposite. Faces and walking feel easy, and theorems feel hard.","children":[]},{"text":"This raises a question about language. This is because GOFAI describes language in formal terms, like a computer program. But computers and humans find different things hard. Since they differ, describing language like a computer program may be a **false trail**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p032":[{"text":"Fixed categories and rules are often **fuzzy** in real life. People have known this for a long time.","children":[]},{"text":"**Wittgenstein (1953)** made this point in **Philosophical Investigations**. He used the category of **games**. Board games, card games, ball games and Olympic games are all games. But when you look closely, no single feature is shared by every game.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because games are linked by **family resemblance** (overlapping similarities with no one shared feature). One pair of games shares one feature. Another pair shares a different feature. Since the features overlap like this, the category holds together without a strict definition.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p033":[{"text":"Many things in language are **probabilistic**. Wittgenstein's games show why a category can work without one fixed definition.","children":[]},{"text":"**Wittgenstein (1953)** compared games feature by feature. Chess and noughts and crosses are not amusing in the same way. Patience has no competition between players. Ball games have winning and losing, but a child throwing a ball at a wall and catching it does not. Skill in chess is different from skill in tennis. Ring-a-ring-a-roses is amusing, but many other features are gone.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because similarities crop up and disappear from one game to the next. They make a complicated network that is **overlapping and criss-crossing**. Some are overall similarities, and some are similarities of detail. Since membership comes from overlap, it can be graded rather than all or nothing.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p034":[{"text":"Many psychologists produced results about **family resemblances**, **similarity-based generalization** and **hunches**. Those results made a lot of cognition seem less suited to formal, rational rule following.","children":[]},{"text":"**Roger Shepard** produced results like this in **perception**. **Eleanor Rosch** did it in **categorization**. **Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky** did it in **decision-making**. Many others did too.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because their results showed people relying on similarity and gut judgments. Those judgments did not follow a simple ideal rational procedure. Since so much cognition works this way, language might too.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p001":[],"m1-lecture07-p002":[{"text":"People make **speech errors** because many things are active in the system at once while they plan what to say. This is because **noise**, **frequency**, other active material and **similarity** can all give the wrong item a boost. When the wrong item gets enough of a boost, it comes out instead of the intended one.","children":[]},{"text":"Speech errors are interesting to psycholinguists because they reveal how speech is planned. This is because an error can split a word into parts, move a sound, or swap in a related word. The way it goes wrong tells you which units the system works with.","children":[]},{"text":"**Fromkin**'s title \"Speech errors as linguistic evidence\" is gutsy. This is because linguists can study **competence** (knowledge of the language) apart from **performance** (actual speaking). From that view, an error is just a performance mistake to throw away. Fromkin says the opposite. Since the structure of an error compares directly with the intended form, errors count as real evidence about language.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p003":[{"text":"Speech errors happen when the wrong item gets more activation than the intended one. This is because in **spreading activation** models, activation spreads from each word to related items. There are four key concepts.","children":[]},{"text":"**Noise** is not just literal acoustic noise, although that causes problems too. It also means activation going up and down in different parts of the system.","children":[]},{"text":"**Frequency** matters because common words are easier to activate than rare ones. This seems to affect many levels of speech production.","children":[]},{"text":"**Other sources of activation** also count. What you just said stays active for a while. What you are about to say is already active. Things you are thinking about but trying not to say may be active too.","children":[]},{"text":"**Similarity** matters because activation spreads more easily between related items. Items can be similar in meaning (semantic), in grammar (syntactic) or in sound (phonological).","children":[]},{"text":"Since all four can add up on one competitor, that competitor can win and come out by mistake.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p004":[{"text":"A **tip-of-the-tongue (TOT) state** is when you know the meaning of a word but cannot say it. This is because the word's meaning and sound are only partly activated. That activation stays below the **threshold** (the level needed to say the word).","children":[]},{"text":"Less frequent words are the ones most likely to get stuck. This is because their sound forms are harder to activate.","children":[]},{"text":"Priming helps because a **prime** (a related word you read first) adds activation to the missing sound form. That extra push can get the form over the threshold.","children":[]},{"text":"**Lise Abrams** reviewed an example where a definition asked for goods traded illegally. The answer was \"contraband.\" People stuck in a TOT state read a prime and then tried again. Primes sharing the first syllable, like \"contact,\" \"connotation\" and \"conscious,\" fixed about **50 percent** of TOT states. Primes sharing the middle syllable helped a medium amount. Primes sharing the final syllable helped about as little as unrelated words. Unrelated words fixed only about one quarter. Since the first syllable helped most, overlap at the start of the word matters most.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p005":[{"text":"**Slips of the tongue** are sorted by which way a sound moves. Spooner supposedly once said \"Mardon me padam, this pie is occupewed. Can I sew you to another sheet?\" These examples come from Fromkin.","children":[]},{"text":"**Anticipation** is when a sound comes too early. This is because a sound planned for later jumps ahead. \"Week long race\" came out as \"reek long race,\" since the r from \"race\" showed up early.","children":[]},{"text":"**Perseveration** is when a sound sticks around too long. This is because an earlier sound carries into a later spot. \"John gave the boy\" came out as \"John gave the goy,\" since the g from \"gave\" carried forward.","children":[]},{"text":"**Metathesis**, or an **exchange**, is when two sounds trade places. \"Keep a tape\" came out as \"teep a cape.\" The k and the t swapped, and the rest of each word stayed put.","children":[]},{"text":"The type depends only on how the produced form relates to the intended form. It does not matter whether the result makes sense.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p006":[{"text":"The spreading activation idea most clearly at work in these slips is **other sources of activation**. This is because what you just said and what you plan to say are both active while you talk.","children":[]},{"text":"In anticipation, the planned sound is active too early. So the r in \"reek long race\" fills a spot before its own.","children":[]},{"text":"In perseveration, the spoken sound is still active. So the g in \"John gave the goy\" fills a spot after its own.","children":[]},{"text":"In an exchange, two sounds in the same plan get in each other's way. So each one lands in the other's spot.","children":[]},{"text":"The other ideas still help decide what happens. **Similarity** and **frequency** affect which candidates compete well. **Noise** (activation going up and down) affects which one gets picked.","children":[]},{"text":"Since planning always runs ahead of the sound you are saying, the wrong active sound can sometimes slip in.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p007":[{"text":"A **mondegreen** is a **slip of the ear**, where a listener hears different words than the ones said. This is because the listener splits the sounds into words in the wrong places.","children":[]},{"text":"The name itself comes from a mishearing. \"Laid him on the green\" was heard as \"Lady Mondegreen.\" In a song, \"Hold me closer, tiny dancer\" gets heard as \"Hold me closer, Tony Danza.\" The real words are \"Tiny Dancer,\" like the song title. **Andrew Nevins** is analyzing crowdsourced mondegreens from kissthisguy.com.","children":[]},{"text":"This happens because speech comes as one continuous stream. So the listener has to figure out the **word boundaries** (where one word ends and the next begins). \"The good candy came anyways\" and \"The good can decay many ways\" sound almost the same. The listener uses **context** to tell them apart.","children":[]},{"text":"Mondegreens are common in songs. This is because songs make **prosody** (the rhythm and melody of speech) less helpful. Since prosody helps less, context and familiar words take over, and sometimes they are wrong.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p008":[{"text":"A **malapropism** like this is a hearing mistake more than a speaking mistake. This is because the speaker says exactly what they mean to say.","children":[]},{"text":"First, the speaker hears a word like \"congenital\" (present from birth). Then the speaker decides the intended word must be a more familiar one, like \"congenial.\" After that, the speaker says \"a congenial heart defect\" and fully means it.","children":[]},{"text":"This is **top-down processing** (using the words you already know to make sense of what you hear). This is because a familiar word helps organize the sound. The familiar word beats the less familiar one. Other examples include \"lettuce days,\" \"decaffeinated him\" and \"Utopian tubes tied.\"","children":[]},{"text":"So two errors can sound equally strange but come from different places. A slip happens while speaking. A malapropism comes from hearing the word wrong earlier.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p009":[{"text":"Speech errors show that single sounds are units in **speech planning**. When **Fromkin** wrote the paper, people argued about whether the **phoneme** (or phone, a single speech sound) was a planning unit.","children":[]},{"text":"Whole consonant **clusters** might have always moved together. Then errors would look like \"gish frotto\" or \"clesh freer water.\" Fromkin found that clusters get broken up all the time instead.","children":[]},{"text":"\"Fish grotto\" came out as \"frish gotto.\" This is because the r left the gr cluster. The r then joined the f in the first word.","children":[]},{"text":"\"Fresh clear water\" came out as \"flesh queer water.\" This is because the r and the l swapped. The f and the k stayed where they were.","children":[]},{"text":"\"Split pea soup\" came out as \"plit spea soup.\" Only the s moved.","children":[]},{"text":"Since one sound can move out of a cluster by itself, the single sound must be a unit the planner works with.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p010":[{"text":"**Fromkin (1971)** made an early version of the model Menn describes. It shows each step from a meaning to a spoken **utterance**.","children":[]},{"text":"It starts with the **meaning to be conveyed**. Then a syntactic structure generator and a semantic feature generator build **syntactic-semantic structures**. Then an intonation contour generator adds **primary stress** and **intonation**.","children":[]},{"text":"Next the **lexicon** gives words with their features and the order of their segments. This makes strings of segments divided into syllables.","children":[]},{"text":"Then **morphophonemic rules** and **phonetic rules** fill in the exact sounds. Last, **motor commands** to the muscles produce the utterance.","children":[]},{"text":"The steps matter because each one is a separate stage. So an error can mess up one stage while the others stay fine. Since each stage can fail on its own, different errors tell you about different stages.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p011":[{"text":"The **basic model** of speech production has five levels. This is because making an utterance involves several different jobs.","children":[]},{"text":"The **message level** is what you want to get across. The **functional level** picks **lemmas** (word identities before their final form) and gives them **semantic roles**. The **positional level** puts them in order in a clause. The **phonological encoding level** fills in the sounds. The **speech gesture level** gets the speech movements ready.","children":[]},{"text":"Following Menn, the first two levels come first. The sound slips like \"reek long race\" were already phonological encoding errors.","children":[]},{"text":"The levels are kept apart because the everyday word \"word\" mixes them up. Picking a fitting word is one job. Picking its grammatical form is another. Putting its sounds together is a third. Since these are separate jobs, different errors can come from different levels.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p012":[{"text":"The **message level** is where you decide what you want to get across. The same event can turn into different messages. This is because the audience changes what you choose to say.","children":[]},{"text":"Take \"I won a bunch of money on the game Sunday.\" With a fan of the losing team, their feelings may change how much you play up the win. With someone not interested in football, you may need to explain why it matters. In a tweet, the short public post changes which details you keep.","children":[]},{"text":"The event is the same each time. Only the purpose and the chosen details change.","children":[]},{"text":"The message level is mysterious and hard to study experimentally. This is because giving everyone the same event does not make them all mean the same thing.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p013":[{"text":"A picture book like \"Frog, where are you?\" helps control what speakers see, but not what they mean to say. This is because it is an **elicitation paradigm** (a task made to draw out speech) that gives everyone the same scene.","children":[]},{"text":"The scene shows a boy, a dog and frogs. Since everyone sees the same thing, researchers can compare the descriptions.","children":[]},{"text":"The intended message is still hard to pin down. This is because one speaker may focus on the boy while another focuses on the dog or the frogs. Speakers may also guess differently about what the listener knows. Age, culture and audience may change how the message gets built.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the scene is fixed but the message is not, the method controls the input more than the message.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p014":[{"text":"We cannot say the functional level picks finished \"words.\" This is because one error reveals that the tense gets added later.","children":[]},{"text":"The speaker meant \"Where'd I put it when I brought it in.\" The speaker said \"Where'd I bring it when I put it in.\" The verbs switched places.","children":[]},{"text":"But the moved verb came out as \"bring,\" not \"brought.\" This is because \"Where'd\" already has the past-tense helper did in it. A verb after did takes the plain form. So the tense was fitted to the verb's new spot.","children":[]},{"text":"If \"brought\" had been picked as a finished word, it would have moved unchanged. PUT looks the same in both forms, so BRING is the verb that gives this away.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the tense came later, the speaker picked the **lemma** BRING (the word's identity before its final form). The term lemma is borrowed from mathematics, where it means a proposition in an argument.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p015":[{"text":"People so often say the opposite of what they mean because opposites are very closely linked. This is an **error in lemma selection**, where the wrong word gets picked.","children":[]},{"text":"One speaker said \"I really like to\" and then fixed it to hate. One said bottom but meant the top of the stack of books. One said \"This room is too damn hot\" and fixed it to cold. One said oral but meant the written part of the exam.","children":[]},{"text":"Each pair is about the same thing. Hot and cold are both about temperature. Top and bottom are both about position. Oral and written are both kinds of exam.","children":[]},{"text":"The wrong word still fits the sentence. This is because it is the same kind of word as the right one. Since it fits, the sentence sounds normal even though the meaning is flipped.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p016":[{"text":"People say the opposite because activation spreads easily from a lemma to its antonym. This is because **antonyms** (words with opposite meanings) are actually alike in several ways.","children":[]},{"text":"Antonyms are **semantically related**, so they are about the same thing. They are also **strongly associated**, so thinking of one brings up the other. In the spreading activation framework, this is like a perfect storm.","children":[]},{"text":"Antonyms can also take on the **same semantic roles**. They are in the **same word class**, so they can take on the same syntactic roles. This means the rest of the speech production system can handle the wrong word.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the antonym gets lots of activation and still fits, saying \"hot\" for \"cold\" makes a normal sentence with the opposite meaning.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p017":[{"text":"A **blend** is one word made by mixing parts of two competing words. **Lewis Carroll** made blends on purpose and called them portmanteau words.","children":[]},{"text":"In The Hunting of the Snark, Carroll explained \"frumious.\" You plan to say both \"fuming\" and \"furious\" without deciding which comes first. If you lean a little toward fuming, you say \"fuming-furious.\" If you lean toward furious, you say \"furious-fuming.\" With a perfectly balanced mind, you say \"frumious.\"","children":[]},{"text":"**Fromkin** quoted Carroll because he said blends can happen in natural speech. Carroll also proposed an idea about how they happen.","children":[]},{"text":"Fromkin found that complete blends like \"frumious\" are rare in normal speech. A blend of [fjurijəs] and [fjumiŋ] is more likely to come out as [fjumijəs] or [fjuriŋ]. This is because the first syllables are identical. Since the words already share a start, they join where they overlap.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p018":[{"text":"**Spontaneous blends** happen when two words that mean nearly the same thing are both active. This is because neither word wins outright, so parts of both come out. \"I swindged\" mixes **switch** and **changed**. Other blends mix **mainly** and **mostly**, **bother** and **trouble**, **swinging** and **hip**, **spirant** and **fricative**, and **player** and **athlete**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Fromkin** explained how this happens. The speaker picks words by matching **semantic features** to the meaning. When there are several options, both go into a **buffer** (a temporary storage compartment) along with their sounds. Then either one gets picked or the two get blended. These errors seem to support Carroll's assumptions.","children":[]},{"text":"There are almost always several possible words in speech production. This is because choosing among options is a normal part of speaking. Since options are almost always there, blends show competition that happens all the time.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p019":[{"text":"**Semantic roles** (also called functional roles) say who did what in an event. They stay the same even when the sentence order changes.","children":[]},{"text":"\"The plums in the icebox were eaten by me\" has the order Undergoer, Location, Verb, Agent. \"I have eaten the plums in the icebox\" has the order Agent, Verb, Undergoer, Location. Both describe the same event.","children":[]},{"text":"At the functional level, lemmas get slotted into the roles. The verb is TO EAT. The **agent** (the one doing it) is ME. The **undergoer** (the thing it happens to) is PLUM. The icebox is the **location**.","children":[]},{"text":"The order still matters for the grammar. This is because the lemma ME becomes \"I\" in the active sentence. It becomes \"me\" after \"by\" in the passive one. That choice is made later, at the **positional level**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p020":[{"text":"By the end of the **functional level**, a lot is already done. The speaker has decided on a **message**. The speaker has picked the right **lemmas**. The speaker has linked those lemmas to **semantic roles**.","children":[]},{"text":"But the speaker is still nowhere near putting a clause together, much less saying it out loud. This is because none of these steps sets the word order or the sounds. Whether the sentence is active or passive is still open. How the pronouns come out is still open. The full sound sequence is still open.","children":[]},{"text":"Since only the plan is ready, later levels still have to order the lemmas and turn them into speech.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p021":[],"m1-lecture07-p022":[],"m1-lecture07-p023":[{"text":"Very often **speech errors go unnoticed**. This is because of **top-down effects** in speech perception. Listeners use context and expectations to figure out the intended message. So a real error can slip by. The same kind of process is assumed for many malapropisms and mondegreens.","children":[]},{"text":"Some \"errors\" are made on purpose for **style**. **Paul Krugman** wrote \"the upper hand is on the other foot,\" quoting Hot Shots Part Deux. It mixes \"the upper hand\" with the shoe being on the other foot.","children":[]},{"text":"A deliberate error can be **more memorable** than the correct version. This is because the odd mix grabs attention to the wording itself. The listener spots both familiar phrases, which makes it funny.","children":[]},{"text":"Since some errors are made on purpose, it matters whether an error was intentional before using it as evidence about speech.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p001":[],"m1-lecture08-p002":[{"text":"After the functional level, three more levels turn the chosen words into speech. This is because the words still need a grammatical order, a sound plan and mouth movements. The **positional level** puts the words into a grammatical structure. The **phonological encoding level** turns them into a string of **phonemes** (speech sounds that tell words apart). The **speech gestural level** has the motor system carry out that string as movements.","children":[]},{"text":"Speech errors are evidence that **abstract units** are real. This is because errors move units like **phonemes**, **lemmas** and **noun phrases** in regular ways. Nobody can hear these units directly. Since errors treat them as units anyway, the units are useful in the theory.","children":[]},{"text":"Collected errors can be sorted by the level where they went wrong. Purely **observational research** also has weak spots, so **experimental methods** are needed too.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p003":[{"text":"In \"Where'd I bring it when I put it in?\", the functional level already knows the verbs and who does what. This sentence was earlier evidence that **lemmas** (words chosen for meaning and grammar, before their sounds) are useful.","children":[]},{"text":"The functional level knows three things. There are two verbs, PUT and BRING. I/ME is the **Agent** (the doer) of both verbs. IT is the **Undergoer** (the thing acted on) of both verbs.","children":[]},{"text":"The functional level does not yet know everything else. It does not set the **temporal relationship** between the verbs, which the word when expresses. It does not set the question form or the verb tenses.","children":[]},{"text":"The **positional level** is where all of this gets expressed in syntax. This is because the positional level builds the actual sentence structure. Since the roles alone give no word order or tense, a separate level has to add them.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p004":[{"text":"The positional level builds a sentence frame with **slots**, and each slot decides the verb's form. Without the question steps, the frame is roughly \"Where'd I [verb, root form] it when I [verb, past] it in?\"","children":[]},{"text":"This is because English grammar asks for different forms in each clause. The first clause needs the **base form** of the verb. That clause took real syntax, with **Wh- movement** and an **auxiliary verb**. The when clause needs the **past form**. The frame also holds the other **particles** the grammar needs.","children":[]},{"text":"The error happens while the lemmas from the functional level are being put into the slots. BRING went into the root form slot, so it came out as bring. PUT went into the past slot, so it came out as put. Since each verb takes the form of its slot, the sentence still sounds grammatical.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p005":[{"text":"\"A language needer learns\" is similar to the bring and put error because two lemmas got switched. The speaker meant \"a language learner needs.\" This is because LEARN and NEED got swapped while being put into the positional level. The frame stayed mostly the same, a noun phrase and then a verb.","children":[]},{"text":"It is different because of the noun. The noun is actually a **verb plus an affix**. Learner is the verb learn plus -er. In the error, need gets the -er instead, so it becomes needer. Adding -er to a verb is **derivational morphology** (making a new word out of an existing one).","children":[]},{"text":"Since only the roots moved, this error happened inside words, not between whole words.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p006":[{"text":"In \"a language needer learns,\" the roots NEED and LEARN switched slots, but the endings stayed put. This is because the endings belong to the slots, not to the words.","children":[]},{"text":"The noun slot is built as a **verb plus -er**. That -er is a **derivational affix** (an ending that makes a new word). It turns a verb into a noun for the person doing it. The verb slot asks for the **third person singular** form, which adds -s. That -s is **inflectional morphology** (an ending the grammar requires).","children":[]},{"text":"The tree has a noun phrase (NP) and a verb phrase (VP). Need landed in the noun slot, so it got -er and became needer. Learn landed in the verb slot, so it got -s and became learns.","children":[]},{"text":"If the whole words learner and needs had swapped, their endings would have moved with them. Since the endings stayed, the affixes are called **stranded**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p007":[{"text":"\"He did that easy enoughly\" puts the ending **-ly** on the wrong word. The speaker meant \"He did that easily enough.\"","children":[]},{"text":"This is because -ly should have gone on easy, making easily. Instead it went on enough, making the odd form enoughly. Easy was left without any ending.","children":[]},{"text":"The questions are how this error is similar to \"a language needer learns,\" and how it is different. To figure that out, compare the intended words with the produced words one at a time.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p008":[{"text":"\"Easy enoughly\" is similar to \"a language needer learns\" because both have a **stranded affix** (an ending that stays in its slot while a different root lands there). Here the affix is **-ly**, which makes an **adjective** into an **adverb**.","children":[]},{"text":"It is different because something also went wrong while setting up the **positional level** itself. This is because the frame's order changed. In the intended frame, the adverb slot comes first, built as adjective plus -ly. The plain adjective slot comes second. In the starred error frame, the adjective slot comes first and the adverb slot comes second.","children":[]},{"text":"Easy filled the first slot and enough filled the second slot. So enough got the -ly. Since the frame itself flipped, this error is not only about filling slots, unlike \"needer.\"","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p009":[{"text":"Errors like needer and enoughly are evidence that **derivational morphology** is real. This is because **affixes** seem to float around at the positional level.","children":[]},{"text":"Another view says derived words are just separate stored words. In that view, they are linked to their roots in some rule-like way. The lexicon would hold sets like needy, need and needless, or unlearn, learn and learner. Then affixes would not need their own representation. Speakers would not need quirky rules for building common words either.","children":[]},{"text":"But speakers make **non-words** like \"needer\" and \"enoughly\" on the fly. These could not come from a stored list. So the errors suggest three things. **Lemmas and affixes** are stored **separately**. **Affixes have a slot** in the positional level. **Derived words** can be built by a **grammatical process**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p010":[{"text":"Adding an affix at the positional level does not finish the job. This is because the sounds of the new word still have to be worked out.","children":[]},{"text":"Sometimes the sounds are just added on. Easy /’i.zi/ becomes easily /’i.zIl.i/. Enough /i.’nəf/ becomes enoughly /i.’nəf.li/.","children":[]},{"text":"Sometimes the base word changes too. The word certificate is /sər.ˈtI.fɨ.kɨt/. The word certification is /ˌsər.tɨ.fɨ.ˈkeI.ʃən/. The word recertification is /ˈri.sər.tɨ.fɨ.ˌkeI.ʃən/. Across these words, the **vowels change** and the **stress moves**.","children":[]},{"text":"The positional level only says something like \"add -er\" or \"make this adjective into an adverb\". Since that instruction does not give the sounds, a later level still has to work out how to say the word.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p011":[{"text":"**Phonological encoding** (turning lemmas, affixes and particles into a string of phonemes) worked fine for \"easy enoughly.\" This is because the speaker did not say \"easy /inəvɪli/\" or mess up the sounds in some other way. So that error happened earlier.","children":[]},{"text":"Errors at this level mix up the **order of phonemes**. Fish grotto came out as gish frotto. Chomsky and Halle came out as Chomsky and Challe. With this ring I do wed came out as with this wing I do red.","children":[]},{"text":"Menn calls these errors **\"neat.\"** This is because they follow English **phonotactic patterns** (rules for which sound sequences are allowed). As far as anyone knows, they are also **clearly produced**. Since only the order went wrong, the wrong sounds still come out clean.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p012":[{"text":"At the **speech gestural level**, the motor system carries out the planned phonemes as **gestures** (movements). Errors here make speech **unclear or imprecise**. This is because the mistake is in the movement, not the plan. So these errors are **not necessarily \"neat,\"** unlike phonological encoding errors.","children":[]},{"text":"**Many errors are ambiguous.** Ad hoc came out as odd hack. In sounds, /æd hɑk/ became /ɑd hæk/. /æ/ is a low front vowel, as in bat. /ɑ/ is a low back vowel, as in hot.","children":[]},{"text":"This error could come from two levels. At the phonological encoding level, it could be **metathesis** (two sounds switching places in the plan). At the gestural level, it could be a **miscuing of tongue position**. Since both give the same result, the error alone cannot tell which level failed.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p013":[],"m1-lecture08-p014":[{"text":"These errors split into word form errors and sound errors. This is because each one changed a different kind of unit.","children":[]},{"text":"Buyed in, for bought in, is a word form error. The speaker put the regular past ending on a verb whose past is irregular. This is **morphological regularization** (using the regular pattern where an irregular form belongs). Arising came out as \"arriving.\" One consonant changed in a word that sounds very similar.","children":[]},{"text":"In \"It's pretty close to here,\" close was said as [kloʊz]. The last consonant became voiced, but this close normally ends in a voiceless one. Thoroughly came out as \"throfly,\" with several sounds changed.","children":[]},{"text":"Since each error changes a different unit, compare the intended and produced sounds first. Then the unit that changed tells which level went wrong.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p015":[{"text":"Some of these errors move whole words, and others move single sounds. This is because different units can get switched.","children":[]},{"text":"In \"Wash my teeth and brush my face,\" the **verbs** wash and brush switched. The objects teeth and face stayed in place.","children":[]},{"text":"Following vowel came out as \"vollowing fowel.\" The first consonants **switched**. Seat heaters came out as \"heat seaters.\" Sounds moved, but the plural ending stayed on seaters. Speech-language pathology came out as \"speech plath... speech plang.\" The speaker made attempts and repairs, and those count as evidence too.","children":[]},{"text":"Some errors bring in a familiar sequence. Lana del Rey came out as \"Marina del Rey.\" \"For all intensive purposes\" puts different words into a familiar phrase. \"It's in the stimulus\" is another collected error.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p016":[],"m1-lecture08-p017":[{"text":"Saying \"a hundred-dollar room\" in a talk about hot yoga is an error in picking the word for the meaning. The speaker said \"you enter a hundred-dollar room and you do yoga.\"","children":[]},{"text":"This is because the context changes what fits. In a talk about cost, a hundred-dollar room would be normal. In a talk about **hot yoga**, the hundred could be about the room's heat, not its price. So dollar is a real word, but it does not fit what was meant.","children":[]},{"text":"The rest of the sentence is fine. The clause is **grammatical**, and the words are easy to say. Since the grammar and sounds are fine, the error is in **choosing the word**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p018":[{"text":"\"Law and Order SUV\" switched the last two letters of the title SVU. This is because **SUV** is a familiar vehicle abbreviation with the same three letters. **SVU** is part of the show's title.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the produced form is a real, familiar sequence, a competing familiar form may have been **selected**. So the error should not be assumed to be a simple motor slip. The result is still easy to say.","children":[]},{"text":"To analyze it, start with the exact change, SVU becoming SUV. Then ask which familiar forms were competing.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p019":[{"text":"\"He cooked his kitchen,\" said for chicken, mixes up sound and meaning. This is because kitchen and chicken are close in both ways.","children":[]},{"text":"**In sound**, the words are very similar. Their first and middle consonants show up in opposite positions. **In meaning**, both have to do with cooking.","children":[]},{"text":"The grammar did not break. Both words are nouns, and both fit the slot after his. So the sentence keeps its **grammatical frame**.","children":[]},{"text":"Since sound and meaning both play a part, the analysis has to look at the word forms and the situation.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p020":[{"text":"Yes, what observers **expect** can change which errors they **hear and report** in **observational studies**. This is because people listen for the kinds of errors they expect. Learning about speech production changes what they expect. So it can change what they notice and what they write down.","children":[]},{"text":"To figure out where an error happened in the model, the **situation** matters. This is because the analyst has to rebuild what the speaker meant to say. A form can be fine by itself but wrong for what the speaker meant.","children":[]},{"text":"Since both the observer and the context shape the record, a collection of errors is not a neutral sample.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p021":[{"text":"Observers miss many speech errors, sometimes whole classes of them, because of how **speech perception** works. Philosophers of science call this **theory-dependent observation**.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because listeners have three biases. They are bad at hearing sounds **\"halfway\" between two phonemes**, especially **consonants**. They are **biased against** hearing **phonotactically illegal strings** (sound sequences the language does not allow). They are **biased toward** hearing unclear sounds **as words**. A sound between \"giss\" and \"kiss\" sounds more like \"kiss.\"","children":[]},{"text":"**Experimental methods** and careful measurement can fix some of these problems. They cannot fix all of them. Since perception filters what gets reported, the reported errors are not the full set.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p022":[],"m1-lecture08-p023":[],"m1-lecture09-p001":[],"m1-lecture09-p002":[{"text":"People name pictures of objects faster when the object's name is a frequent word. **Oldfield and Wingfield (1965)** found this when they asked people to name pictures of objects. The example line drawings come from a later set of drawings by Snodgrass and Vanderwart (1980), not from the original study.","children":[]},{"text":"They found a **log-linear relationship** (the times fall on a straight line once frequency is put on a logarithmic scale) between frequency and response time. So the more frequent the name, the shorter the response time.","children":[]},{"text":"There is a catch, because frequency is also related to **word length**, even in this tiny sample. This is because frequent words tend to be shorter. So a frequent name is usually also a short name. That means either frequency or length could be making naming faster.","children":[]},{"text":"This kind of problem is called a **confound** (a second variable that changes along with the one being tested). Since length changes along with frequency, length has to be controlled before frequency can be called the cause. A web-based tool lets researchers check this relationship with numbers.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p003":[{"text":"A **lexical database** lets researchers look up the real properties of words instead of guessing. This is because it collects information about many words in one place. The **English Lexicon Project** is one such database.","children":[]},{"text":"It gives two kinds of information. The first is **lexical characteristics** (properties of words, such as frequency and length). The second is **behavioral data** from naming and lexical-decision tasks. The homepage has separate links for making word and nonword lists. It also has links to trial-level lexical-decision and naming data.","children":[]},{"text":"This matters because a list that seems \"frequent\" might also differ in length. A researcher might miss that by intuition. The database makes that hidden difference show up before the words are chosen. Since the properties are measured, the researcher can fix the difference while designing the stimuli.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p004":[{"text":"The query tools can be used in two ways, depending on where the researcher starts.","children":[]},{"text":"The first way is to **generate a list** of items with specific lexical characteristics. This is because sometimes a study needs words in a certain range of frequency or length. The researcher sets the range, and the database finds words that fit.","children":[]},{"text":"The second way is to **submit a list** of items to retrieve their lexical characteristics. This is because sometimes the words were already chosen for another reason, such as belonging to a semantic category. The researcher gives the words, and the database returns their properties.","children":[]},{"text":"Both ways do the same job. They make the properties of the stimuli clear. Since the properties are clear, an unintended difference between conditions can be caught. So stimulus design often goes back and forth between searching and checking.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p005":[{"text":"A database query has two parts, the **fields** to report and the **constraints** on which words get in.","children":[]},{"text":"Fields are the properties the database reports for each word. The researcher ticks checkboxes for **length** and for frequency measures. This is because getting both lets the researcher see whether length changes along with frequency.","children":[]},{"text":"Constraints are limits on a property. Minimum and maximum boxes set the range for the chosen frequency field. Only words inside that range make it into the list.","children":[]},{"text":"For a frequency study, the goal is words that differ in frequency but not in length. The frequency limits find candidate words. Then the returned lengths tell the researcher whether more picking is needed. Since the database only follows the criteria, the researcher still decides the comparison.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p006":[{"text":"Word frequency in English drops off very sharply, and length goes up as frequency goes down. This is because a few words are used far more than all the rest.","children":[]},{"text":"To see this, the 200 most frequent words in an old corpus were put into ten \"bins\" of twenty words each. The mean frequency drops very dramatically from the most frequent bin to the next. It keeps falling across the later bins. This steep drop is **Zipf's law** (a small number of words make up a large share of all usage).","children":[]},{"text":"The second pattern is that length is **inversely related** to frequency (as one goes up, the other goes down). The most frequent bins have the shortest words on average.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the most frequent words are also the shortest, picking frequent words means picking short words. That is what makes frequency and length hard to separate.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p007":[{"text":"High-frequency words are much shorter than low-frequency words picked at random. **Fewer than 25%** of high-frequency words are as long as the middle low-frequency word.","children":[]},{"text":"This comes from comparing **467 high-frequency (HF) words** with **467 low-frequency (LF) words**. The HF words were the 467 most frequent words. The LF words were picked at random.","children":[]},{"text":"A **boxplot** (a summary of a whole set of values) shows each group's word lengths. The **median** (the middle value) of the LF group sits higher than the median of the HF group. The difference covers most of each group, not just one odd word.","children":[]},{"text":"This means picking LF words at random does not get rid of the length difference. This is because random picking keeps the natural link between frequency and length. Since the link stays, any naming difference could still come from length.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p008":[{"text":"The way to fix the length problem is to stop picking low-frequency words at random.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because HF words are generally shorter than LF words. So random LF words will always come out longer.","children":[]},{"text":"Instead, the researcher picks **long-ish HF words** and **short-ish LF words**. This brings the two groups to about the same length. The groups still differ in frequency. This is called **matching** on length while manipulating frequency.","children":[]},{"text":"Matching only changes the words in the experiment. It does not say that frequency and length are unrelated in the language. The researcher still has to check that the final groups differ in frequency and agree in length. Since length is now the same in both groups, any naming difference can be tied more directly to frequency.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p009":[{"text":"Frequency still affects naming even when length is held the same. **Oldfield and Wingfield** found a **robust frequency effect** after matching their high- and low-frequency words for length.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because matching took length out of the comparison. If length were the real cause, the naming difference should have disappeared. The naming difference stayed. So they concluded that **frequency effects are not merely length effects in disguise**.","children":[]},{"text":"Much of psycholinguistics works this way. Researchers try to show that an effect is not just an effect of some **confounding variable**. A confounding variable is one that changes along with the variable of interest. Since controlling the confound did not remove the effect, frequency matters beyond length.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p010":[{"text":"Careful matching on many dimensions is good design, but it has costs. **Cutler (1981)** called this \"Making up materials is a confounded nuisance.\"","children":[]},{"text":"**Cortese and Simpson (2000)** did a very good matching job. They matched regular and irregular words on many properties. These included frequency, familiarity, imageability, length and sound and spelling measures.","children":[]},{"text":"The first cost is that the chosen items may be somehow \"odd.\" Their statistics may also be inappropriate. This is because the frequency-length confound is a fact about English and most languages. So picking words to avoid it creates a somewhat **artificial stimulus environment**, even when done well.","children":[]},{"text":"The second cost is that very few items may be left. This study had ten words per cell. That is small for a word naming experiment. Since every extra constraint removes words, heavy matching can leave strange and tiny word sets.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p011":[{"text":"Psycholinguists do not have to be as crafty about design as some other researchers. This is because most interesting effects in psycholinguistics happen **below the level of conscious awareness**.","children":[]},{"text":"For example, a person naming line drawings does not usually think about word frequency or length. The person just names the objects. The frequency and length effects still show up in the naming times.","children":[]},{"text":"Researchers studying **racial stereotyping** or **altruism** have to be craftier about experimental design.","children":[]},{"text":"So in **norming studies** (studies that collect ratings of words), researchers simply ask people to rate words on some dimension. They just hope for the best. Some studies still add **fillers** (extra items mixed in with the test items). Since people are not aware of these processes, a simple task can still measure them.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p012":[{"text":"Many tasks measure things that participants do not consciously control. These measures are **dependent variables** (the outcomes the researcher measures).","children":[]},{"text":"**Eye-tracking** records where and when people look. It is very revealing in speech production studies. **EEG** and **fMRI** both measure brain activity. A **tachistoscope** from the 19th century presented stimuli briefly.","children":[]},{"text":"Brain activity is useful mainly because participants usually cannot control it directly. This is because people usually cannot choose how their brain responds. So suppose two kinds of stimuli produce different brain responses, even with no overt task. That suggests the stimuli are different to the participant.","children":[]},{"text":"**Reaction time** and **accuracy** are also unlikely to be under direct control in most experiments. Since participants cannot steer these measures, they reveal how the stimuli are really processed.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p013":[],"m1-lecture09-p014":[{"text":"Naming is slower when the objects in a set are related in meaning. **Belke, Meyer and Damian (2005)** found this using **cyclic semantic blocking** (naming the same sets of objects over and over).","children":[]},{"text":"In **homogeneous** sets, the names were related. In **heterogeneous** sets, the names were unrelated. Naming was slower in related sets once the objects had already been seen.","children":[]},{"text":"They ran three experiments to find where this slowdown comes from. **Experiment 1** found the blocking effect after the objects had been named once. **Experiment 2** showed the objects at the same time. It found blocking in naming latency and gaze duration, but not significantly in the gaze-to-speech lag. **Experiment 3** found the slowdown also hit new names from the same categories.","children":[]},{"text":"The study has nice design features. It changes how objects are presented, adds eye tracking and adds new items.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p015":[{"text":"The **WEAVER++** model by Roelofs only roughly lines up with the general sentence-production model. This is because WEAVER++ focuses on activation for single words, not whole sentences.","children":[]},{"text":"WEAVER++ has **conceptual**, **syntactic** and **word-form** levels. The general model has a message level, a functional level and a positional level. Then come phonological encoding and articulation.","children":[]},{"text":"The syntactic level is where they differ most. In WEAVER++, syntactic information is not \"positional.\" Instead, it is single-word information such as number and gender.","children":[]},{"text":"The conceptual level and the **lemma** level matter most for blocking. A lemma is the word candidate with its syntactic information, such as number and gender. Word-form encoding happens later, after a lemma is chosen. Since blocking is about choosing words, it fits the lemma level.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p016":[{"text":"Spreading activation can make naming slower, even though it sounds like it should make naming faster.","children":[]},{"text":"In a **spreading-activation model**, related concepts are linked. Activating **duck** also activates features such as animal, bird and swim. Those features link to other animals, such as **fish**, **snake** and **mouse**. Each concept then passes activation to its lemma.","children":[]},{"text":"A naive view says a whole block of similar pictures should speed up naming. This is because every name in the block gets extra activation.","children":[]},{"text":"The model explains why naming can get slower instead. This is because naming means choosing one name over the others. A related set makes the target more active. It also makes the competing names more active. Since the competitors are active too, choosing the right one takes longer.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p017":[{"text":"Activating related words makes it harder to pick the right word. This is because speech production has two separate parts, **activation** (how active a word is) and **selection** (choosing the one lemma to say).","children":[]},{"text":"Spreading activation from **duck** to **fish** means activating one helps activate the other. That causes a problem when the task is to select the correct lemma from many active ones.","children":[]},{"text":"**WEAVER++** says selection time depends on a **ratio**. The top of the ratio is the activity of the most active lemma. The bottom is the overall activation of all lemmas. When the total goes up, the ratio goes down, so selection takes longer.","children":[]},{"text":"Feedback from the **category level** adds **collateral activation** (extra activation for other items in the same category). Since that raises the total, lemma selection slows down.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p018":[{"text":"The same pictures were named in both related and unrelated blocks. This is because the researchers wanted to compare the same item in two contexts.","children":[]},{"text":"The experiment ran for **8 cycles**. In each cycle, all of the stimuli were presented.","children":[]},{"text":"**Homogeneous (HOM)** blocks had related items, such as \"duck\", \"mouse\", \"fish\", \"snake\". **Heterogeneous (HET)** blocks had unrelated items, such as \"duck\", \"saw\", \"chair\", \"bus\".","children":[]},{"text":"The same stimulus, such as \"duck\", appeared in HOM and HET blocks in alternating cycles. So duck is not a HOM item or a HET item by itself. It counts as HOM or HET because of the other items in its block.","children":[]},{"text":"This lets researchers compare two things. They can see how naming times change across cycles. They can also see how HOM and HET times differ.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p019":[{"text":"There are two effects happening in the naming times at the same time.","children":[]},{"text":"The first is a big **identity priming effect** (faster naming of an item that was named before). This is because naming the same pictures again makes them quicker. Both HOM and HET times drop sharply after the first cycle.","children":[]},{"text":"The second is a **semantic blocking effect** (slower naming in related blocks). In later cycles, HOM responses are slower than HET responses.","children":[]},{"text":"The two effects do not contradict each other. This is because they come from different comparisons. Identity priming compares one item across cycles. Semantic blocking compares HOM and HET within the same cycle. Since the comparisons are different, a condition can get faster over time and still be slower than the other condition.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p020":[{"text":"The blocking effect is not likely to come from visual similarity. The worry was that related objects might look alike, so blocking could happen during **visual object recognition**.","children":[]},{"text":"Belke and colleagues gave two responses. First, the stimuli were **normed** so that objects in the same category were not more visually similar on average than objects in different categories.","children":[]},{"text":"Second, they ran a **control experiment** that blocked visual similarity. It found different results. Blocking by visual similarity showed up in the first block of trials. Semantic blocking showed up only after the first cycle.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because the two kinds of blocking had different timing. Semantic blocking did not appear in the first cycle. Since the timing was different, the effects in Experiment 1 are not likely due to visual similarity.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p021":[{"text":"Blocking still happens when objects are seen all at once. Belke and colleagues tested this because of a study by **Hermens and colleagues**. That study showed several objects simultaneously and found no blocking effect.","children":[]},{"text":"The worry was that maybe something is odd about seeing objects one after another, in serial. If so, blocking might only come from serial viewing.","children":[]},{"text":"To check, participants viewed **displays of four items simultaneously**. They then said the names in serial, one after another. One display had duck, snake, fish and mouse.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because the task separates seeing from speaking. The participant sees the whole set at once. The participant still has to pick and say each name in turn. Since blocking appeared in naming latency and gaze duration, it does not depend on serial viewing.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p022":[{"text":"Belke and colleagues measured **eye movements** while people named the four objects. This is because eye movements break naming into smaller pieces of time.","children":[]},{"text":"The participant looks at one object. Then the eyes move to the next object. The name of the first object is said after the eyes have already moved on.","children":[]},{"text":"The pieces have labels. **GD** is **gaze duration** (the time spent looking at an object). **Art** is when the speaker starts saying the name. The **lag** is the time between the end of looking and the start of the name.","children":[]},{"text":"Since each piece is measured separately, researchers can see which piece differs between HOM and HET displays.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p023":[{"text":"There are two key dependent variables, **gaze duration** and **lag**. Each one tracks a different stage of naming.","children":[]},{"text":"Gaze duration is the time spent looking at an object. It matters because there is evidence that **lemma selection** (choosing the word) is coordinated with eye movements. So a change in gaze duration suggests a change in lemma selection.","children":[]},{"text":"Lag is the time after the eyes leave an object and before its name starts. Later processes happen here, such as **phonological encoding** (building the sound form) and **gesture planning** (planning the speech movements). This is because these processes can happen without visual support once the eyes have moved on.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the two measures track different stages, they can show which stage the semantic effect hits.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p024":[{"text":"Where a speaker looks lines up with what the speaker says. **Griffin, Bock and colleagues** made demonstrations of this **eye-mouth coordination**.","children":[]},{"text":"One example is accurately labeling a patient in \"The horse is kicking a cow.\" The **agent** (the one doing the action) is the horse. The **patient** (the one affected by the action) is the cow.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because eye tracking records where the speaker looks while the sentence is being said. So the eye record can be lined up with the spoken words.","children":[]},{"text":"Since looking and speaking line up, eye tracking can reveal when each part of an event matters for building a sentence.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p025":[{"text":"Of the three eye-tracking measures, only gaze duration shows a blocking effect.","children":[]},{"text":"**Overall speech duration** gets shorter over the course of the experiment. It does not show a blocking effect.","children":[]},{"text":"**Gaze duration** shows a blocking effect, but only after the first cycle. People look longer at objects in HOM displays.","children":[]},{"text":"**Lag** shows no effect. Numerically, the blocking effect in lag seems to go in the opposite direction. This difference is not significant.","children":[]},{"text":"This is confusing at first because the measures can change over the cycles. That change comes from practice, not from semantic context. A measure can get shorter across cycles without differing between HOM and HET. Since only gaze duration differs between HOM and HET, the semantic cost shows up in looking time.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p026":[{"text":"The gaze-duration result places semantic blocking at **lemma selection**. This is because each measure maps onto a different stage of production.","children":[]},{"text":"**Speech duration** maps onto the **gesture level**. It drops over the experiment but shows no blocking.","children":[]},{"text":"**Gaze duration** maps onto **lemma selection**. It shows blocking after the first cycle. Longer looking in HOM displays fits more competition among related lemmas.","children":[]},{"text":"**Lag** maps onto **phonological encoding**. It shows no significant blocking. So there is no evidence of a cost after the eyes leave the object.","children":[]},{"text":"Since only the lemma-selection measure shows blocking, lemma selection is where the cost is clearest.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p027":[{"text":"Yes, blocking generalizes to new items in the same category. **Experiment 3** tested this by introducing **new items** in **Cycle 5**.","children":[]},{"text":"In Cycle 5, the repetition priming effect went away. This is because the new items had never been named before. So overall performance slowed down dramatically.","children":[]},{"text":"Even so, the blocking effect was still there. HOM naming stayed slower than HET naming.","children":[]},{"text":"The new items can be compared to the items in Cycle 1. In Cycle 1, nothing had been named yet, and there was no blocking. In Cycle 5, the new items came from categories that had already been named. Since blocking appeared for these new items, it comes from the **semantic category**, not from repeating a particular list.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p028":[{"text":"Belke and colleagues isolate the blocking effect by ruling out three other explanations.","children":[]},{"text":"It is not **visual recognition**. This is because the stimuli were matched for visual similarity, and a control experiment gave a different result.","children":[]},{"text":"It is not **phonological encoding** (building the sound form). This is because the words are not similar in sound. So sound overlap cannot slow them down.","children":[]},{"text":"It is not **item-specific**. This is because it generalizes to new items from the same category.","children":[]},{"text":"Each explanation sits at a different point between seeing an object and saying its name. Since all three are ruled out, what is left is **lemma selection** among related words that are all active.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p029":[{"text":"Each rival explanation for blocking has its own test, and the tests point to **lemma selection**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Visual recognition** is ruled out in two ways. The stimuli were normed and matched. A control experiment also tested visual similarity directly.","children":[]},{"text":"**Phonological encoding** is ruled out, but with a question mark. The words are not phonologically similar. Also, gaze duration is affected, but eye-speech lag is not. The question mark is there because timing measures only support an interpretation. They do not directly observe the stage.","children":[]},{"text":"A **specific effect of repeating lists** is ruled out because the effect generalizes to new items. Since each rival explanation fails its test, the effect is placed at lemma selection.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p030":[{"text":"A good experiment idea starts with the question \"Couldn’t it just be …?\" This is because most effects could have another cause.","children":[]},{"text":"For **stimulus manipulations**, many word features are correlated with frequency. These include **length**, **age of acquisition**, **concreteness** or **imageability** and **contextual diversity**. So frequency effects might just be a catch-all for these other properties. Researchers can test this by matching a feature, as Oldfield and Wingfield did with length.","children":[]},{"text":"For **experimental manipulations**, even a simple blocking study involves many processes. Two of them are **object recognition** and **response selection**. Belke and colleagues tested such processes with controls, new items and eye tracking.","children":[]},{"text":"Since each alternative predicts a different result, asking the question leads straight to a new comparison.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p031":[],"m1-review-p001":[],"m1-review-p002":[{"text":"The **Midterm 1** review describes **ten short essay questions**. About **100 to 250 words** should be enough to answer each one. Each question has **three or four gradeable items**. This is because each item can earn its own credit, so an answer can earn **partial credit** for every part it explains well.","children":[]},{"text":"The exam is **open book**. This is because you can use your materials during the exam, so you do not need to memorize names and dates. Since the work is explaining concepts, practice answering a focused question in a connected paragraph or two.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p003":[],"m1-review-p004":[{"text":"Consonants are described by three **features**. A feature is one separate property of how a sound is made.","children":[]},{"text":"**Place of articulation** is where the vocal tract is constricted. The chart puts place on the horizontal axis, from the front to the back of the vocal tract.","children":["This is because bilabial sounds are made at the lips, so they sit toward the left. Velar and more back places sit farther right."]},{"text":"**Manner of articulation** is how the airflow is controlled. It runs from complete closure to open airflow. A **plosive** is a sound with a complete closure.","children":[]},{"text":"**Voicing** is the timing of **vocal fold vibration** (the buzzing of the vocal folds). Voicing can start before or during the closure, or after it. Within each pair, the right-hand symbol is the voiced member.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p005":[{"text":"**/p/** and **/b/** have the same place and manner, but they contrast in **voicing**. Both are **bilabial** plosives, so the lips form a closure and then release it. This is because the place and manner stay the same, so only the voicing timing can differ. The two sounds differ in the timing of phonation relative to the release of the closure. The same kind of voicing comparison applies to /t/ and /d/, even though that pair is made at a different place. So we can talk about the **voiced stops**, /b/, /d/ and /g/, as one group.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p006":[{"text":"A **minimal pair** is two words that differ in meaning because of a difference in one speech sound. This is because the rest of the word stays the same, so that one sound must carry the change in meaning. A minimal pair establishes that the two sounds are **contrastive**, meaning they belong to different **phonemes** (sound categories that can change word meaning).","children":[]},{"text":"Caught and cot serve this purpose for a speaker who keeps their vowels apart. The surrounding consonants stay the same, so the vowel difference carries the contrast. For a speaker who merges those vowels, that pair does not show two vowel phonemes. So the evidence is the contrast in meaning, not just two pronunciations that sound different.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p007":[{"text":"In English, /b/ and /p/, /d/ and /t/, and /g/ and /k/ each differ in **voicing**. This is because each pair keeps the same place and stop manner, so only the voicing category changes. Comparing the pairs makes clear that voicing is the shared feature behind several contrasts. The bilabial, alveolar and velar stops occur at different places, yet the voiced and voiceless relation recurs at each place.","children":[]},{"text":"The evidence has to come from the language being studied. Two phones that exist in a language do not by themselves show a phonemic contrast there. Minimal pairs connect a sound distinction with a difference between words, so they are the test that matters.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p008":[{"text":"Speech is a **continuous** acoustic signal, not a row of separate letters. This is because successive articulations overlap, so the sounds run into each other as the sentence unfolds. The **spectrogram** (a display of frequency over time, where darker areas show stronger energy) of \"A sore back is a real pain\" shows this. Time runs from left to right, and frequency runs up the display.","children":[]},{"text":"Broad frequency patterns change over time, and transitions connect neighboring sounds. A phonemic transcription gives useful categories. Those categories do not match isolated blocks with identical sound shapes in every context. So recognizing the sentence means reading changing patterns, not finding a clean boundary around each letter. The labels and boundary markers above the display only organize that reading.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p009":[{"text":"Hockett's 1955 **analogy** describes the listener as an inspector who identifies eggs from a mess. This is because the eggs stand for the intended sequence of sounds, and the mess stands for the speech signal after production. The eggs are different sizes and colors, and the wringer breaks and mixes them. The inspector stands for the hearer.","children":[]},{"text":"The inspector does not have to put the eggs back together, which would be physically impossible. Instead the inspector identifies which eggs were there from broken yolks, spread whites and bits of shell. So recognizing speech does not need each original sound to be cleanly separated. Overlapping traces can still support identification.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p010":[],"m1-review-p011":[{"text":"**Derivational morphology** is the process that builds new words from existing parts. Those parts are called **morphemes** (the smallest meaningful units of words). This is because each part adds meaning, so combining parts makes a new word. The word **morphology** is built from **morph-**, which comes from Greek and means form, and **-ology**, which means the study of.","children":[]},{"text":"So morphology means the study of word forms. The word **phone** is another morpheme, and **morpheme** itself is built in a similar way. A morpheme is not the same thing as a syllable or a single speech sound. It contributes meaning or grammatical information.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p012":[{"text":"**Derivational patterns** can be productive but still limited. The suffix **-hood** appears in brotherhood, sisterhood and knighthood. This is because each word names a state tied to the noun, but the meaning is not identical in each one. Brotherhood can describe a shared relation among brothers. Knighthood describes a status that each knight holds.","children":[]},{"text":"Some -hood words behave differently. Neighborhood is an established word, but -hood does a different job there. Friendhood is not used, because English says friendship instead. Enemyhood is only a proposed form, since words like enmity or antagonism cover that concept. So a recognizable affix does not combine equally with every base that could make sense.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p013":[{"text":"**Zero derivation** changes a word's category without adding any visible affix. This is because the word form stays the same, so the new meaning has to come from learned uses. The verb in \"She can dish it out but she can't take it\" depends on a learned use of dish. \"They decided to table the issue\" depends on another learned use, of table.","children":[]},{"text":"Associations among knife, dish, spoon, fork and table do not make their verb uses interchangeable. \"Forked tongue\" combines an object-related shape with an established figurative meaning. Reading these as literal references to utensils or furniture would miss the intended meanings. So an unchanged word form does not guarantee an unchanged meaning.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p014":[{"text":"Adding an affix can change the pronunciation of the word it attaches to. This is because the stress and vowels can shift when the new material is added. **Certificate**, **certification** and **recertification** differ in their stress patterns and in some vowels. This is one reason English spelling is hard, since a closer match to speech would not always make words easier to read.","children":[]},{"text":"The noun and verb forms of **record** and **invite** also differ in pronunciation. This is because the main change is stress, so the prominent syllable moves even though the spelling stays the same. The two uses of **entrance** are pronounced differently too. Their spelling is similar, but they are not related by derivation.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p015":[{"text":"**Inflection** changes the grammatical form of a word while keeping the same word. This is because the grammatical job changes, but the word is still one lexical item. Plural nouns, third-person singular present verbs and past-tense verbs all show this.","children":[]},{"text":"Knife and knives are two forms of one noun. Knife, knifes and knifed are forms of one verb. Forks and mice both mark plural, and walked and sang both mark past tense.","children":[]},{"text":"The sound change can be irregular, but the grammatical job stays the same. Menn says it would be silly to have separate dictionary entries for the singular and plural forms of nouns. So inflection does not need a new dictionary meaning for each form.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p016":[{"text":"Grammar can require a particular inflected form, but derivation does not have that requirement. This is because counting with **two** makes the following noun plural, whether the plural is regular, as in forks, or irregular, as in knives. The slot stays the same, and different nouns fill it.","children":[]},{"text":"Derivation does not work the same way. The pattern \"Once you verb it, you can un-verb it\" suggests a possible word, but it does not require every verb to have an un- form. This is because an un- form depends on whether a particular derived verb exists or makes sense. So inflection is a grammatical obligation, while a derived word is only a possible new word.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p017":[],"m1-review-p018":[{"text":"A **lexical node** is a unit in a simple network that stands for one word. The nodes LION, TIGER and STRIPES can each receive **activation** (a value that gives how strongly a node is active) from a presented word. This is because a presented word sends evidence to the node that represents it.","children":[]},{"text":"The input display and the lexical nodes are two different levels of description. A visible word supplies the evidence. The node is the word the system could recognize from that evidence. Treating each node as one unit makes it possible to describe how evidence for one word changes the whole system.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p019":[{"text":"**Activation** can be partial before a word is identified. This is because LION contrasts poorly with the noise mask, so the input gives only weak evidence for LION. The LION node can receive some activation even while recognition is still uncertain.","children":[]},{"text":"A graded activation value makes these in-between states clear. The system does not stay inactive until recognition is complete. Its state changes as useful evidence comes out of the noise. Weak input raises a candidate's activity without yet giving enough support for a confident identification.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p020":[{"text":"A **perceptual identification task** measures when a person can recognize an item. The participant presses a button as soon as the item can be identified. This is because the signal becomes strong enough for the network to identify LION. The **signal-to-noise ratio** (the strength of the useful word information compared with the background) decides when that point comes.","children":[]},{"text":"The response time connects the button press to the model's account of activation growing over time. TIGER and STRIPES stay active as alternative nodes, but LION is the one identified. A condition that makes identification easier moves the button press earlier. So researchers can compare the factors that help word recognition.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p021":[{"text":"**Frequent words** are identified faster because experience changes how activation unfolds. This is because each encounter can change the system in a simple way. A word might start with a higher **baseline activation**. It might also have stronger incoming connections, or a lower **recognition threshold** (the amount of activation needed for a response).","children":[]},{"text":"Each mechanism links past experience to faster identification. A higher baseline gives a head start before input arrives. Stronger connections make the same input raise activation more. A lower threshold means less activation is needed for a response.","children":[]},{"text":"Rumelhart and McClelland's 1986 Interactive Activation model simulates these effects. In its activation curves, time runs along the horizontal axis and activation along the vertical. The curve for CAVE rises as input is processed and then falls, while other words stay much less active. These curves describe states inside a model, not measured firing rates.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p022":[{"text":"Seeing a word again soon after seeing it makes it faster to identify. This is because activation falls off more slowly than it rises. If the word is encountered again while activation is still high, its baseline is already higher than usual.","children":[]},{"text":"The first presentation changes the system that receives the second. The falling side of the CAVE curve stays above its earlier baseline. So a repeat during that decline starts from a raised level instead of the resting level. A shorter gap leaves more **residual activation** (activation that remains after the input ends), so the repeat is identified sooner.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p023":[{"text":"Two more effects need relationships among word nodes. These are **neighborhood effects** (how similar a word is to other words) and **related-word priming** (seeing a similar or related word first). This is because the first effect depends on similar words influencing one another. The second depends on one word changing how another word is processed.","children":[]},{"text":"A model of isolated word nodes can only change the activity of one word. So to explain an effect from a different word, the model must specify how the representations interact. Related-word priming can be associative, semantic or phonological. A connected network provides the mechanism for that influence.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p024":[{"text":"**Spreading activation** means that activating one word sends some activity to related words. This is because the network has weighted connections, so activating LION sends activity along the connection to TIGER. Each connection strength gives how strongly two words are associated.","children":[]},{"text":"This network can explain **semantic priming**, where a target is easier to access after a related word. The target is partly activated by the prime, so it is ready sooner when it appears. Association norms show which words are related. A prime can change the target's starting state without being the target.","children":[]},{"text":"A semantic network can link vehicle, car and bus, and it can link fire engine to both vehicles and fire-related concepts. This is because multiple connections let one activated concept influence several related words.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p025":[{"text":"Balota and Lorch tested whether activation can travel through a connecting concept that is never presented. LION and STRIPES are not directly associated, but each is linked to TIGER. This is because the indirect route goes from LION to TIGER and then to STRIPES.","children":[]},{"text":"The comparisons included direct primes, primes that reach the target through a connecting word, neutral primes and unrelated primes. **Mediated priming** is the name for that indirect route. In **lexical decision**, people judge whether the target is a word. Direct primes helped, but mediated priming did not beat the neutral or unrelated primes.","children":[]},{"text":"In **pronunciation**, people read the target aloud. Both direct and mediated priming helped responses. Balota and Lorch proposed that lexical decision hid the indirect benefit. Since mediated priming helped in pronunciation, activation does travel through the connecting concept.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p026":[{"text":"**Mediated priming** happens when a prime helps a target through an intervening association. This is because LION is connected to TIGER, and TIGER is connected to STRIPES, so activating LION can raise activity at STRIPES. LION and STRIPES are not directly associated, so the route through TIGER is the only link.","children":[]},{"text":"TIGER supplies this route without being presented as an extra stimulus. A direct association links a prime to its target in one step. The indirect route needs activity to pass through the network. So facilitation between indirectly related words is evidence about how the network is organized. The same path can be followed from the other end, with STRIPES as the input and TIGER as the connecting node.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p027":[{"text":"Pronunciation was faster after mediated priming than after either control prime. Averaged across the two timing conditions, mediated priming gave a mean of 558 ms. Neutral primes gave 577 ms, and unrelated primes gave 575 ms. Latency here is the time before a participant starts saying the target, so a lower number means an earlier start.","children":[]},{"text":"Direct related primes were fastest, at 549 ms on average. The benefit of mediated priming was 19 ms against neutral primes and 17 ms against unrelated primes. Small differences like these matter because careful control conditions can pick out influences that are hard to notice in everyday speech.","children":[]},{"text":"**Stimulus onset asynchrony** (SOA, the time from the start of the prime to the start of the target) was 250 ms or 500 ms. At 250 ms, mediated priming averaged 558 ms, compared with 570 ms for neutral and 574 ms for unrelated primes. At 500 ms, mediated priming again averaged 558 ms, compared with 583 ms and 576 ms. Since mediated priming was faster at both SOAs, the benefit did not depend on one timing.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p028":[{"text":"**Spreading activation** can also slow naming. This is because activating several related words can make it harder to choose one of them. Activating duck can spread activation to fish, snake and mouse through shared properties and categories.","children":[]},{"text":"One might expect a block of similar items to make naming faster. But a naming task requires selecting one candidate over the others. A semantically homogeneous set activates several related candidates at once. That activity makes the target available, but it also makes its competitors available. So the effect on naming time depends on the selection process, not only on extra activation for the target.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p029":[],"m1-review-p030":[{"text":"In 1861, Pierre Paul Broca presented the case of **Tan**, a patient who had lost the ability to speak after a brain injury decades earlier. This is because Tan could produce only one jargon syllable, yet seemed otherwise to be in possession of his senses. Tan also seemed to understand what was said to him.","children":[]},{"text":"The contrast between speaking and understanding made the case important. Producing speech and understanding speech can be affected differently by brain damage. A serious speaking problem could exist alongside other abilities. So the case pointed toward a more specific explanation than a complete loss of intelligence or all language ability. Broca presented it to the newly established Society of Anthropology, where he was also the founding president.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p031":[{"text":"Tan's brain had a **lesion** (a region of damage) that was large and definitely included the third frontal convolution. That convolution is also called the **inferior frontal gyrus**. This is because the anatomical description places the damage in the left frontal lobe. This observation helped establish **Broca's area**, a part of the left frontal lobe that is important for speech.","children":[]},{"text":"Hughlings Jackson made a criticism in 1874. Finding the damage that destroys speech is not the same as finding where speech is. This is because damage can interrupt a process without showing every operation of the intact system. The preserved brain also shows the injury was extensive, not a sharply isolated speech component.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p032":[{"text":"In the 1870s, German neurologists Lichtheim and Wernicke studied aphasia more systematically. They explained losses of function as **disconnections** between centers. This is because a damaged pathway between two centers can cause one kind of loss without destroying both centers. In Lichtheim's house model, A stands for sound images, B for concepts, and M for movement images.","children":[]},{"text":"A disconnection between A and B would interfere with understanding spoken words. This is because the heard form could not reach its meaning. A disconnection between A and M would interfere with verbatim repetition. This is because the heard form could not reach the speech movements. So the pathway matters as much as the center.","children":[]},{"text":"The triangular diagram joins A, B and M. Lowercase a marks the acoustic input pathway, and lowercase m marks the motor output pathway. Numbered interruptions mark sites whose damage is proposed to cause different impairments. This approach of describing centers for specific functions is an ancestor of **modularity**.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p033":[{"text":"Freud criticized the diagram-drawing approach to aphasia research. This is because he thought it was naive to expect the brain to be organized by psychological categories such as word forms or concepts. He proposed instead that language emerged from fields connecting perceptual and motor abilities to drives.","children":[]},{"text":"This raises a lasting question in cognitive neuroscience. Can brain area functions be related directly to cognitive functions? This is called a **holist critique**. A psychological distinction can be useful even when the biology involves interacting systems rather than one isolated center. So a mental category has to be connected to brain mechanisms through evidence.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p034":[{"text":"Dronkers and colleagues used MRI to measure the damage in Broca's landmark cases more precisely. This is because MRI can show the extent and location of damage. The lesion goes deep and includes the **insula**. It also has much white matter damage in the **superior longitudinal fasciculus**, a pathway that connects the front and back of the cerebral cortex.","children":[]},{"text":"Other work from the same group suggests that the insula, not Broca's area, is strongly associated with **speech apraxia**. Speech apraxia is the production problem Broca described in his patients. So the anatomical evidence changes how the historical case should be read. The case moves from a small surface speech center toward a wider pattern of injury that includes disrupted connections.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p035":[],"m1-review-p036":[{"text":"A **lemma** (the abstract identity of a word before its final grammatical form is set) is selected before the form is fixed. This is because the error \"Where'd I bring it when I put it in\" swaps the two verbs, but the swapped verb takes a new form. The intended sentence was \"Where'd I put it when I brought it in.\" BRING appears as \"bring\" after \"Where'd I\", not as \"brought\". So the lemma BRING was selected, and then expressed in present form.","children":[]},{"text":"An exchange of two fully formed words would carry \"brought\" along unchanged. This is why the error supports separating lemma selection from the processes that set the final form. The first clause already has \"did\", so the verb after it takes the base form. PUT looks the same in its base and past forms, so BRING gives the clear evidence.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p037":[{"text":"People often say the opposite of what they mean because antonyms are strong competitors during **lemma selection**. This is because antonyms are semantically related and strongly associated, so activation spreads easily from a lemma to its antonym. Antonyms also belong to the same word class and can take the same semantic and syntactic roles.","children":[]},{"text":"So the rest of the speech production system can handle the wrong word. \"Hot\" and \"cold\" both describe temperature and can complete the same sentence frame. A wrong candidate can still form an ordinary sentence. Semantic similarity here does not mean the same meaning. It means sharing a domain and a close relation within that domain. So a big change in meaning can follow from a small, grammatically smooth substitution.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p038":[{"text":"**Semantic roles** stay the same across sentences that describe the same event. This is because each participant keeps its relation to the event even when its grammatical position changes. \"The plums in the icebox were eaten by me\" and \"I have eaten the plums in the icebox\" describe the same basic event.","children":[]},{"text":"In the first sentence, the plums are the undergoer, the icebox is the location, and the speaker is the agent. The passive places undergoer and location before the verb and agent. The active starts with the agent, then the verb, undergoer and location. So the agent is the one doing the eating, no matter where that participant appears.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p039":[{"text":"**Functional planning** has done three things. It has decided on a message to convey, selected the appropriate lemmas, and linked each lemma to a semantic role. This is because the speaker needs the word choices and their roles before a sentence can be built. For an eating event, the system can identify EAT, the one acting, and the thing eaten.","children":[]},{"text":"These choices do not yet decide whether the sentence is active or passive, how the pronouns come out, or the full sound sequence. So the plan is organized but is not yet a spoken clause. Separating these steps explains how a lexical choice can move while later processes still arrange the sentence.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p040":[{"text":"In \"Where'd I bring it when I put it in?\", the **functional level** supplies two verb lemmas, BRING and PUT, with their participants. The speaker is the agent of both verbs, and IT is the undergoer of both. This is because those role assignments identify who takes part in each event.","children":[]},{"text":"A grammatical sentence also has to express how the two events are related. The positional level sets the clause pattern, the question structure, and the verb forms that tense requires. So knowing which participant performs an action is different from knowing where each word goes in the spoken sequence. A speaker can keep the right participants while putting a verb in the wrong place.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p041":[{"text":"The question frame is \"Where'd I [verb, root form] it when I [verb, past] it in?\" This is because the first clause has the auxiliary did, so its main verb takes the base form. The clause after **when** needs a past-tense form. The positional level supplies both requirements as well as the slots for the selected lemmas.","children":[]},{"text":"If BRING and PUT enter the wrong slots, their forms can still fit the slots they occupy. The verb in the first slot is a base form, and the verb in the second slot is a past form. So the identity of a lemma separates from the inflection the frame requires. The error can be thought of as happening while the positional slots are being filled with the activated lemmas.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p042":[{"text":"In \"a language needer learns\", two lemmas switched places, but the endings stayed with their grammatical jobs. This is because the noun is built from a verb root plus the derivational affix **-er**, and the final verb gets third-person singular **-s**. The structure has a noun phrase, NP, and a verb phrase, VP.","children":[]},{"text":"Derivational morphology changes the kind of word, as -er turns a verb into an agent noun, such as learner. Inflectional morphology expresses a grammatical requirement, as -s does in learns. Both affixes are still readable in the error. So the roots changed places, but each affix stayed attached to the job it does.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p043":[{"text":"\"He did that easy enoughly\" also has a **stranded affix** (an affix left without its expected root). This is because -ly is the derivational ending that turns an adjective into an adverb. But this error also changed how the positional structure was set up. Two trees contrast an adverb followed by an adjective with an adjective followed by an adverb.","children":[]},{"text":"Changing which root fills the adverb position produces \"enoughly\", while \"easy\" is left without its expected ending. This is because the -ly ending goes with the adverb position. The starred structure puts the adverb position after the adjective position. So this error changes the frame itself, not only two lemmas inside an unchanged frame.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p044":[],"m1-review-p045":[{"text":"Oldfield and Wingfield asked people to name pictures of objects. They found a **log-linear relation** between frequency and response time. This means the points fall roughly on a straight line when frequency is put on a log scale. The relation means more frequent names were named faster. The line drawings come from Snodgrass and Vanderwart (1980).","children":[]},{"text":"Frequency is confounded with length here. This is because frequent words tend to be shorter, so a frequent name is usually also a short one. Either property could explain the faster naming. A **confound** (a second variable that changes along with the one being tested) makes the result ambiguous. So length has to be controlled before frequency can be called the cause.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p046":[{"text":"English **word frequency** is highly uneven. The 200 most frequent words in an old corpus (a large collection of text) were sorted into ten groups of twenty. The mean frequency falls sharply from the most frequent group to the next. This is because a small number of words account for a large share of usage. The steep drop fits **Zipf's law** in the ranked vocabulary.","children":[]},{"text":"Length moves in the opposite direction. The highest-frequency groups contain shorter words on average, so length is inversely related to frequency. Selecting very frequent words therefore selects many short words. So a random low-frequency comparison will usually differ in more than frequency.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p047":[{"text":"Researchers can avoid this **confound** by choosing the words deliberately instead of at random. This is because high-frequency words are generally shorter than low-frequency words, so random low-frequency words would almost always be longer. Researchers pick relatively long high-frequency words and relatively short low-frequency words.","children":[]},{"text":"These choices come from the overlapping parts of the two length distributions. The chosen words are less typical of their frequency groups, but they make the contrast possible. The check is whether the final groups still differ on frequency while matching on length. So matching keeps the frequency difference and holds length similar. Matching applies only to the chosen words. Across English as a whole, frequent words are still generally shorter.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p048":[{"text":"Oldfield and Wingfield later matched their high- and low-frequency words for length. They still found a robust frequency effect. This is because if length alone explained the result, matching length should have removed the naming difference. Since the difference survived, frequency has an effect beyond length.","children":[]},{"text":"A **confound** is handled by changing the materials so that it predicts something different from the proposed explanation. Here frequency stays different while length becomes similar. That separation gives the surviving frequency effect its value. So much of psycholinguistics tries to show that an effect is not just another confounding variable.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p049":[],"m1-review-p050":[{"text":"Belke, Meyer and Damian (2005) used **cyclic semantic blocking** to study picture naming. Participants repeatedly named sets of objects whose names were either semantically related or unrelated. This is because the design compares naming in related sets with naming in unrelated sets. Naming was slower in the related sets once the objects had already been encountered. Repetition gives its own speed up, so the cost of related context still stands out.","children":[]},{"text":"The experiments used changes in presentation, eye tracking and new items to find where the cost arises. Experiment 1 showed that the blocking effect appeared after the objects had been named once. Experiment 2 presented the objects all at once. It found blocking in naming latency and gaze duration, but no significant blocking in the gaze-to-speech lag. Experiment 3 showed that the cost generalized to new names from the same categories.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p051":[{"text":"In speech production, activation and selection must be kept apart. This is because spreading activation from duck to fish means activating one can help activate the other. That becomes a problem when the task is to select the correct **lemma** (the word identity chosen for speech) from many active ones.","children":[]},{"text":"The WEAVER++ model, a model of word production, says selection time depends on the most active lemma compared with the overall activation of all lemmas. So when feedback from the category level adds collateral activation to other members of the category, the target is less dominant. The speaker then takes longer to select the correct lemma.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p052":[{"text":"Belke, Meyer and Damian's 2005 study used **cyclic semantic blocking** to examine picture naming. Participants named sets of objects over and over, with names that were either semantically related or unrelated. This is because the repeating cycles let the study compare the same objects in the two kinds of set. Naming slowed in the related sets once the objects had been encountered.","children":[]},{"text":"Experiment 1 found that the blocking effect appeared after the objects had been named once. Experiment 2 presented the objects simultaneously, and it found blocking in naming latency and gaze duration. It found no significant blocking in the gaze-to-speech lag. Experiment 3 found the cost also generalized to new names from the same categories. The paper calls related sets homogeneous and unrelated sets heterogeneous.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p053":[{"text":"Speech production requires keeping activation and selection apart. This is because a related word can become active without being the word that gets chosen. Spreading activation from duck to fish means activating one can help activate the other. That causes trouble when the task is to choose the correct lemma from many active ones.","children":[]},{"text":"The **WEAVER++** model, a model of word production, assumes that selection time depends on the most active lemma compared with all lemmas together. Category feedback adds collateral activation to items from the same category. Then the target is less dominant, so lemma selection slows.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p054":[{"text":"Gaze duration shows a blocking effect, but only after the first cycle. This is because looking at an object is coordinated with selecting its name. So a longer gaze in homogeneous sets fits more competition among related lemmas. So the selective gaze effect supports a **lemma selection** account of semantic blocking.","children":[]},{"text":"The gaze-to-speech lag shows no effect. This is because the numbers go in the opposite direction, but the difference is not significant. Overall speech duration decreases over the experiment, but it shows no blocking effect. A missing lag effect gives no parallel evidence for a cost after the gaze leaves the object.","children":[]},{"text":"The stages match up this way. Speech duration matches the gesture level, gaze duration matches lemma selection, and the lag matches later phonological encoding (building the sound form of the word). The drops across the experiment are related to repetition, not to blocking. The important comparison is whether homogeneous and heterogeneous sets differ within each measure.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p055":[{"text":"Blocking still appears when new items from the same categories are introduced. This is because the effect does not depend on repeatedly naming one particular list of names. In cycle five, new items were introduced, and naming became much slower. The repetition advantage for the old items was lost, so both curves rose sharply.","children":[]},{"text":"Even so, the homogeneous sets stayed slower than the heterogeneous sets. This is because the process is tied to the semantic context, which survived the change in items. The effect therefore **generalized beyond the particular names** that had been practiced. Comparing cycle five with the first cycle helps separate category experience from simply having named a particular object before.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p056":[{"text":"Belke, Meyer and Damian paired each alternative explanation with a **control test**. This is because a test that rules out an alternative makes the blocking effect easier to attribute to lemma selection. Visual recognition is addressed by norming and matching the pictures and by a separate visual-similarity control.","children":[]},{"text":"The phonological alternative is examined by using words that are not phonologically similar and by separating gaze duration from the eye-to-speech lag. The repeated-list alternative is tested by introducing new items from the same categories.","children":[]},{"text":"The generalization result is especially important because it changes the names while keeping the category history. The eye-tracking result divides the naming sequence into measured intervals. So these designs give complementary evidence that the effect is located around lemma selection. The phonological stage stays a question, because the timing measures support an interpretation rather than observing a hidden stage directly.","children":[]}]}};
